Let  $N$  be the midpoint of  $AT$ . We see that  $\angle NAO=\pi/2$ , so  $(AT)$  and  $(ABC)$  are orthogonal. Thus, the inversion  $\phi$  about  $(ABC)$  fixes  $(AT)$ , but it sends  $(BOC)$  to  $BC$ . We then have that
\[\phi(\{A_1,A_2\})=(AT)\cap BC=\{T,D\}\]
where  $D$  is the foot from  $A$  to  $BC$ . We see that  $OT>OD$ , so  $A_1=\phi(T)$  and  $A_2=\phi(D)$ .

(a) Note then that  $A_1$  is the foot from  $A$  to  $TO$ , so the reflection of  $A$  about  $A_1$ , call it  $A'$ , is the harmonic conjugate of  $A$  in  $BC$ . Thus,  $AA_1$  is the  $A$ -symmedian, with  $AA_1$ ,  $BB_1$ ,  $CC_1$  concurring at the symmedian point of  $ABC$ .

(b) Note that  $\phi(AA_2)=(AOD)$ , so we want to show that  $(AOD),(BOE),(COF)$  are coaxial, where  $DEF$  is the orthic triangle of  $ABC$ . Note that the power of the orthocenter  $H$  with respect to these circles are  $HA\cdot HD=HB\cdot HE=HC\cdot HF$ , so  $H$  has equal power with respect to these three circles. This means that these three circles are coaxial with radical axis  $OH$ , so the concurrence point of the three circles  $X$  is on the Euler line. Thus, the concurrence of  $AA_2,BB_2,CC_2$  lies on the image of the Euler line under  $\phi$ , which is actually just the Euler line, so we're done.  $\blacksquare$

