Let  $I$ ,  $I_A$  be the incenter and  $A$ -excenter, with  $H$  the point on  $\overline{BC}$  such that  $\overline{IH} \perp \overline{BC}$ . Suppose that the perpendicular bisector of  $\overline{AE}$  meets  $\overline{AE}$ ,  $\overline{DG}$ ,  $\overline{DF}$  at  $M$ ,  $V$ ,  $N$  respectively. We want  $VM = NM$ . 

Note that  $VNGF$  is cyclic from   $$ \angle VGF = \angle DGF = \angle BDF = \angle VNF, $$   so it suffices to prove  $\overline{ID}$  is a symmedian in  $\triangle DFG$ , where  $I$  is well-known to lie on  $\overline{DM}$ . If  $S = \overline{FG} \cap \overline{BC}$ , this is equivalent to  $\triangle SDI_A \sim \triangle IHD$ . Assume  $C$  lies between  $B$  and  $S$ . By Power of a Point, we can calculate \begin{align*}SD^2 &= SG \cdot SF = SB \cdot SC = (SD+BD)(SD-CD)  \implies SD &= \frac{BD \cdot CD}{BD-CD} = \frac{(s-b)(s-c)}{b-c}.\end{align*} Thus   $$ \frac{SD}{I_AD} = \frac{\frac{(s-b)(s-c)}{b-c}}{\frac{[ABC]}{s-a}} = \frac{1}{b-c} \cdot \frac{(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)}{[ABC]} = \frac{r}{b-c} = \frac{IH}{DH} $$   and we are done.  $\blacksquare$

It's well known that there exists some homothety centered at  $D$  which sends the midpoint of  $\overline{AE}$  to the incenter  $I$  of  $\triangle ABC$  and sends  $E$  to the incircle touchpoint with side  $\overline{BC}$ <details><summary>.</summary>this is 90% of the difficulty of the problem</details> Let  $\ell$  be the line through  $I$  parallel to  $\overline{BC}$ .

It suffices to show that if lines  $FD$  and  $GD$  intersect  $\ell$  at  $X$  and  $Y$  respectively, the midpoint of  $\overline{XY}$  is  $I$ .

Let  $P$  and  $Q$  be the intersections of lines  $FD$  and  $GD$  with  $(ABC)$ , respectively. By Reim's theorem,  $BCQP$  is an isosceles trapezoid. In fact, we will show, via length calculations, that line  $PQ$  is exactly halfway between parallel lines  $BC$  and  $XY$ . In particular, the distance from  $Q$  to line  $\overline{BC}$  is
\begin{align*}
& \sin ( \angle QDC ) \cdot QD 
= ~ & \sin (\angle DFG) \cdot \frac{DC \cdot BD}{DG} 
= ~ & \frac{DC \cdot BD}{\frac{DG}{\sin (\angle DFG)}} 
= ~ & \frac{DC \cdot BD}{2r_A} 
= ~ & \frac{r}{2},
\end{align*}
as desired. <details><summary>elaboration on the last step</summary>Maybe there's some really simple reason I'm missing for why  $r \cdot r_A = DC \cdot BD$ , but here's a convoluted proof: by incenter-excenter lemma,  $IBI_AC$  is cyclic; let  $I'$  be the reflection of  $I$  across the perpendicular bisector of  $\overline{BC}$ . Then,  $I'BI_AC$  is cyclic with perpendicular diagonals; the equation we're trying to prove follows by power of a point on the diagonals.</details>

Thus, a homothety of factor  $2$  centered at  $D$  sends  $P$  to  $X$  and  $Q$  to  $Y$ ; thus, the perpendicular bisector of  $\overline{PQ}$  is sent to the perpendicular bisector of  $\overline{XY}$ . But the perpendicular bisector of  $\overline{PQ}$  is the perpendicular bisector of  $\overline{BC}$ ; a homothety of factor  $2$  centered at  $D$  sends the perpendicular bisector of  $\overline{BC}$  to the line through  $I$  perpendicular to  $\ell$ . In particular,  $I$  is the midpoint of  $\overline{XY}$  as desired.

