solution_md:
Why do all solutions use roots of unity? ;) Anyway, here's my solution: We will prove the following crucial Lemma first-**LEMMA** Suppose for some odd prime  $p>3$ , and integers  $a,b$  and  $0<k<p$  we have  $p^k \mid a^2+ab+b^2$ , then   $$ (a+b)^p \equiv a^p+b^p \pmod{p^{k+1}} $$  <u>*Proof of Lemma*</u>
If  $p$  divides any one of  $a,b$  then the given condition forces both to be divisible by  $p$ , in which case the result is obvious (Since both sides are divisible by  $p^p$ , so they must be divisible by  $p^{k+1}$  for  $0<k<p$ ). From now on assume that both are not divisible by  $p$ . Note that this also means that  $p \nmid a-b,a+b$  (else the given condition again forces  $p \mid a,b$ ). We induct on  $k \geq 1$ . First consider  $k=1$ . Since  $p \mid (a+b)^p-(a^p+b^p)$  (by Binomial Theorem), so we wish to show that   $$ \frac{1}{p} ((a+b)^p-(a^p+b^p)) \in p\mathbb{Z}_p $$  Let's work modulo  $p$ , and take  $x \equiv ab^{-1} \pmod{p}$ . Then the given condition is  $x^2+x+1 \equiv 0 \pmod{p}$ , which gives us \begin{align*} \frac{1}{p} ((a+b)^p-(a^p+b^p)) &\equiv \frac{1}{p} ((x+1)^p-(x^p+1)) \pmod{p}  &\equiv \frac{1}{p} ((-x^2)^p-(x^p+1)) \pmod{p}  &\equiv -\frac{1}{p} \cdot \frac{x^{3p}-1}{x^p-1} \pmod{p}  \end{align*}
Here, since we have assumed  $p \nmid x-1$ , so  $\gcd(x^p-1,p)=1$  (by FLT). So we just need to show that  $p^2 \mid x^{3p}-1$  if  $p \mid x^3-1$  (Since  $p \mid x^2+x+1$  gives  $p \mid x^3-1$ ). But this is a direct consequence of LTE. So the result is true when  $k=1$ .

Now suppose the result is true for  $k-1$  (where  $k>1$ ). We will show that it holds for  $k$  also. Since  $p^{k-1}$  also divides  $a^2+ab+b^2$ , so by induction hypothesis  $p^k \mid (a+b)^p-(a^p+b^p)$ . So our task is reduced to proving   $$ \frac{1}{p^k} ((a+b)^p-(a^p+b^p)) \in p\mathbb{Z}_p $$  We again work modulo  $p$ , and use  $x \equiv ab^{-1} \pmod{p}$ . Then the given condition is  $x^2+x+1 \equiv 0 \pmod{p^k}$ , which gives us \begin{align*} \frac{1}{p^k} ((a+b)^p-(a^p+b^p)) &\equiv \frac{1}{p^k} ((x+1)^p-(x^p+1)) \pmod{p}  &\equiv \frac{1}{p^k} ((-x^2)^p-(x^p+1)) \pmod{p}  &\equiv -\frac{1}{p^k} \cdot \frac{x^{3p}-1}{x^p-1} \pmod{p}  \end{align*}
Again, we just need to show that  $p^{k+1} \mid x^{3p}-1$  if  $p^k \mid x^3-1$ , and LTE does the rest.  $\Box$ ------

Return to the problem. Applying our Lemma for  $k=2$ , we get that  $(a+b)^p-(a^p+b^p)$  is a multiple of  $p^3$  if  $p^2 \mid a^2+ab+b^2$ . So it suffices to find such pairs with  $a,b<p$  for infinitely many primes. Note that by Weak Dirichlet (provable using cyclotomic polyomials), there are infinitely many primes  $p$  of the form  $3k+1$ . Let  $p$  be such a prime. Then  $\phi(p^2)=p^2-p=3k(3k+1)$ . Taking  $g$  to be a primitive root  modulo  $p^2$ , we get that  $\text{ord}_{p^2}(g^{k(3k+1)})=3$ . By [Thue's Lemma](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thue%27s_lemma), there exist  $0<m,n<p$  such that   $$ mg^{k(3k+1)} \equiv \pm n \pmod{p^2} $$  Here we cannot have  $m \equiv n \pmod{p^2}$  (since then  $m=n$  must hold, which gives  $g^{k(3k+1)} \equiv \pm 1 \pmod{p^2}$ , a contradiction). Also  $m \equiv -n \pmod{p^2}$  is also not possible (as  $0<m+n<2p<p^2$ ). Now, cubing both sides, we have  $m^3 \equiv \pm n^3 \pmod{p^2}$ . Thus,  $p^2$  must divide  $m^2 \pm mn+n^2$  (since  $m \not \equiv \pm n \pmod{p^2}$ ). If the sign is  $+$ , then taking  $a=m,b=n$  works. Else, if  $p^2 \mid m^2-mn+n^2$ , then  $a=|m-n|$  and  $b=\max(m,n)$  works.  $\blacksquare$ REMARKS: I basically did this problem, while doing [this one](https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c6h2041877p14505338), so my solution to the Lemma is essentially the EDIT part at the end. My original solution there can also be easily accustomed to prove the Lemma, but would be a bit longer, and so I haven't used it here.

candidates:
The main claim is as follows: **<span style="color:#f00">Claim: </span>** For  $p \equiv 1\pmod 3$ , the polynomial  $x^2 + x + 1$  divides  $(x + 1)^p - 1^p - x^p$  with multiplicity at least  $2$ . 

We extend this division statement to the complex numbers. Obviously  $x = 1$  works; now set  $x = \omega$  where  $\omega$  is a third root of unity. We see that \begin{align*}
    (\omega + 1)^p - 1 - \omega^p &= (-\omega^2)^p - \omega^p - 1 
    &= -\omega^2 - \omega - 1 = 0,
\end{align*} as desired. We do a similar calculation to show that  $\omega^2$  works as well.

Thus  $x^2 + x + 1$  divides  $(x + 1)^p - x^p - 1$  with multiplicity at least one. To get the next division, we take the derivative to get the new polynomial  $p(x + 1)^{p - 1} - px^{p - 1}$ . We can check once again that \begin{align*}
    p(\omega + 1)^{p - 1} - p\omega^{p - 1} = p - p = 0,
\end{align*} since  $p - 1 \equiv 0\pmod 3$ . As a result,  $1, \omega, \omega^2$  are all double roots of  $(x + 1)^p - x^p - 1$ , proving the claim.  $\square$ Since  $\binom{p}{1}, \binom{p}{2}, \cdots, \binom{p}{p - 1}$  all contain a factor of  $p$ , we see that we can write \begin{align*}
    (a + b)^p - a^p - b^p = p(a^2 + ab + b^2)^2 \cdot Q(a, b).
\end{align*} Now selecting  $a, b$  such that  $x = ab^{-1}$  and  $x^2 + x + 1 \equiv 0\pmod p$  finishes by taking  $x$  to be  $g^{\frac{p - 1}{3}}$ , where  $g$  is a primitive root of unity.

actual memes

Let's only consider primes  $p \equiv 1 \pmod 3$ . The following observations about such primes solves the problem:

***<span style="color:#00f">Claim:</span>*** The two-var polynomial  $p(a^2 + ab + b^2)^2$  divides the two-var polynomial  $(a + b)^p - a^p - b^p$ .
***<span style="color:#00f">Proof:</span>*** It is sufficient to prove that the 1-var polynomial  $p(a^2 + ab + b^2)^2$  in terms of  $a$  divides the 1-var polynomial  $(a + b)^p - a^p - b^p$  in terms of  $a$  across all complex constants  $b$ . Clearly  $p \mid (a + b)^p - a^p - b^p$  since\[(a + b)^p - a^p - b^p = \sum_{i = 0}^p \binom{p}{i}a^ib^{p - i} - (a^p + b^p) = \sum_{i = 1}^{p-1} \binom{p}{i}a^ib^{p - i}\]and it is easy to see  $p$  divides all binomial coefficients  $\tbinom{p}{i}$  for  $i \neq 0, p$ . Now, to show  $P(a) = (a^2 + ba + b^2)^2$  divides  $Q(a) = (a + b)^p - a^p - b^p$ , we must show that the roots of  $P(a)$  are double roots of  $Q(a)$ , so it suffices to show the roots of  $P(a)$  are the roots of both  $Q(a)$  and  $Q'(a)$ . 

The roots of  $P(a)$  are  $b\omega$  and  $b\omega^2$  where  $\omega = e^{\frac{2\pi i}{3}}$ . Check
\begin{align*}
Q(b\omega) & = b^p[(1 + \omega)^p - \omega^p - 1]&= b^p[-\omega^{2p} - \omega^p - 1]&=b^p[-\omega^2 - \omega^p - 1]&=0
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
Q(b\omega^2) & = b^p[(1 + \omega^2)^p - \omega^{2p} - 1]&= b^p[-\omega^{p} - \omega^{2p} - 1]&=b^p[-\omega^p - \omega^{2p} - 1]&=0
\end{align*}
and that since  $Q'(a) = p[(a + b)^{p-1} - a^{p-1}]$ , check
\begin{align*}
Q'(b\omega) & = pb^{p-1}[(1 + \omega)^{p-1} - \omega^{p-1}]&=0
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
Q'(b\omega^2) & = pb^{p-1}[(1 + \omega^2)^{p-1} - \omega^{2(p-1)}]&=0
\end{align*}
since  $3 \mid p-1$ , so  $w^{p-1} = 0$ . This check finishes the proof of this claim. 

***<span style="color:#00f">Claim:</span>*** If prime  $p \equiv 1 \pmod 3$ , then there exist residues  $a, b$  for which  $p \mid a^2 + ab + b^2$ . 
***<span style="color:#00f">Proof:</span>*** This is equivalent to proving there exist distinct residues  $a, b$  for which  $a^3 \equiv b^3 \pmod p$  since  $p \nmid a - b$ . Let  $g$  be a primitive root, and say  $g^1, g^2, \ldots , g^{p-1}$  generates all  $p-1$  nonzero residues. We can literally select\[(a, b) = \left(g^i, g^{i + (p-1)/3}\right)\]and be done (which is legal due to  $3 \mid p - 1$ ). 

So now to kill the problem, just choose  $p \equiv 1 \pmod 3$  and distinct residues  $a, b$  modulo  $p$  for which  $a^3 \equiv b^3 \pmod p$ , of which there are clearly infinitely many triples.

