Another idea for a construction (I think), similar to the ideas above but without using  $\phi$ : Given two Fibonacci-type sequences  $(a_i)_i$  and  $(b_i)_i$  with  $a_i < b_1 < a_{i+1} < b_2 < a_{i+2}$  for some  $i$ , it follows inductively that the sequences "thread" between each other, i.e.  $a_{j+i-1} < b_j < a_{j+i} < b_{j+1} < a_{j+i+1}$  for all  $j$  (and some fixed  $i$ ):
\begin{align*}
b_3 &= b_2 + b_1 > a_{i+1} + a_i = a_{i+2} 
\Longrightarrow a_i &< b_1 < a_{i+1} < b_2 < a_{i+2} < b_3 
a_{i+3} &= a_{i+2} + a_{i+1} > b_2 + b_1 = b_3 
\Longrightarrow a_i &< b_1 < a_{i+1} < b_2 < a_{i+2} < b_3 < a_{i+3} 
\vdots
\end{align*}

If we can successively add the first two elements of new Fibonacci-type sequences  $A_n = (a_{n,i})_i$  that thread through all previously defined  $A_k = (a_{k,i})_i$  then  $\{A_i\}$  will partition  $\mathbb{N}$ . Start with  $A_1$  given by  $a_{1,1} = 1$  and  $a_{1,2} = 2$  (the normal Fibonacci sequence). Having defined  $A_1, \ldots, A_{k-1}$ , define  $A_k$  by setting  $a_{k,1}$  to be the smallest positive integer not appearing in any of  $A_1, \ldots, A_{k-1}$  and  $a_{k,2}$  to be  $2a_{k,1} - k$ . We just have to show that  $a_{k,2} = 2a_{k,1} - k$  is always available (i.e. not in  $A_1, A_2, \ldots, A_{k-1}$ ) and that  $\{a_{k,1}, a_{k,2}\}$  threads through each of  $A_1, \ldots, A_{k-1}$ . 

Use induction.  $A_2 = \{4,6,10,\ldots\}$ , which threads through  $A_1$  (and is therefore disjoint from  $A_1$ ). Assume  $A_1, \ldots, A_{n-1}$  have been constructed in this way, with the properties that they are disjoint and thread through each other. Define  $A_n = \{a_{n,1}, a_{n,2}, a_{n,3}, \ldots\}$  as outlined above. For  $j \in [n-1]$ , let  $a_{j, i_j}$  be the largest element of  $A_j$  appearing before  $a_{n,1}$ . $\bold{L1}$ :  $\{a_{1,i_1}, a_{2,i_2}, \ldots, a_{n-1, i_{n-1}}\} = \{a_{n,1} - (n-1), a_{n,1} - (n-2), \ldots, a_{n,1} - 1\}$ .

<details><summary>Proof</summary>If the claim were false, then there would exist two elements of some  $A_{j_1}$  in  $\{a_{n,1} - (n-1), a_{n,2} - (n-2), \ldots, a_{n,1} - 1\}$  (with  $1 \le j_1 \le n-1$ ), so by the pigeonhole principle some sequence  $A_{j_2}$  is such that  $a_{j_2, i_{j_2}} < a_{n,1} - (n-1) \le a_{j_1, i_{j_1}-1} < a_{j_1, i_{j_1}} < a_{n,1} < a_{j_2, i_{j_2} + 1}$ . So then  $A_{j_1}$  and  $A_{j_2}$  don't thread, contradicting the IH.</details> $\bold{L2}$ : For each  $j \in [n-1]$ ,  $a_{j, i_j + 2} > a_{n,2}$ .

<details><summary>Proof</summary>From  $\bold{L1}$ ,  $a_{j,i_j} \ge a_{n,1} - n + 1$ . By definition of  $i_j$ ,  $a_{j, i_j + 1} \ge a_{n,1} + 1$ . Then:
\begin{align*}
a_{j,i_j + 2} &= a_{j, i_j + 1} + a_{j, i_j} 
&\ge (a_{n,1} + 1) + a_{n,1} - n + 1
&= 2 a_{n,1} - n + 2 
&= a_{n,2} + 2 > a_{n,2} 
\end{align*}</details> $\bold{L3(A)}$ : For  $j \in [n-1]$ , if  $i_j \ge 2$ , then  $a_{j, i_j + 1} < a_{n,2}$ .

<details><summary>Proof</summary>By definition of  $i_j$ ,  $a_{j, i_j} \le a_{n,1} - 1$ . From  $\bold{L1}$ ,  $a_{j, i_j - 1} \le a_{n,1} - n$ . Then:
\begin{align*}
a_{j,i_j + 1} &= a_{j, i_j} + a_{j, i_j - 1} 
&\le (a_{n,1} - 1) + (a_{n,1} - n) 
&= 2 a_{n,1} - n - 1 
&= a_{n,2} - 1 < a_{n,2}  
\end{align*}</details> $\bold{L3(B)}$ : For  $j \in [n-1]$ , if  $i_j = 1$ , then  $a_{j, i_j+1} = a_{j, 2} < a_{n,2}$ .

<details><summary>Proof</summary>From the construction, it is clear that  $a_{1,1} < a_{2,1} < a_{3,1} < \cdots$ . If  $a_{j, 1}$  is the largest element of  $A_j$  appearing before  $a_{n,1}$ , then it follows that  $a_{k, 1}$  is the largest for  $j \le k < n$  and these are all larger than  $a_{j,1}$  while being bounded above by  $a_{n,1}$ . Then  $a_{j,1} \le a_{n,1} - (n-j)$  so:
\begin{align*}
a_{j,1} &\le a_{n,1} - (n-j) 
&< a_{n,1} - \frac{n-j}{2} 
\Longrightarrow 2 a_{j,1} &< 2 a_{n,1} - n + j 
\Longrightarrow 2 a_{j,1} - j &< 2 a_{n,1} - n 
\Longrightarrow a_{j,2} &< a_{n,2} 
\end{align*}</details>

From  $\bold{L2}, \bold{L3(A)}, \bold{L3(B)}$  and the definitions of  $i_j$  and  $a_{n,1}$ , for each  $j \in [n-1]$  we have  $a_{j, i_j} < a_{n,1} < a_{j, i_j +1} < a_{n,2} < a_{j, i_j + 2}$  so  $A_n$  threads through each of  $A_1, A_2, \ldots, A_{n-1}$  (and is therefore disjoint, so that  $a_{n,2}$  was in fact available).

