Consider the sequence 
\[a-1, a^2-1, a^3-1, \cdots , a^{2a-1}-1,\]
where  $a$  is an odd integer  $\geq 7$ . Each of these terms, by Zsig, has a prime divisor that the others before it don't have. Since for all primes  $p\leq a$  we have  $p|a^{p-1}-1,$  the new prime in  $a^{2a-1}-1$  cannot be a prime less than or equal to  $a$ . This means we can find a prime  $p>a$  that satisfies  $p|a^{2a-1}-1$ . In this case, we claim  $(a,p-a)$  works. It suffices to show that 
\begin{align*}
a^{p-a}+(p-a)^a \equiv a^{p-a}-a^a &\equiv 0\pmod{p} 
\iff a^{p-1}-a^{2a-1} &\equiv 0\pmod{p} 
\iff 1-a^{2a-1}&\equiv 0\pmod{p},\end{align*}
which is true based on how we chose  $p$ .

