WLOG  $P$  is in arc  $AB$ . Note that  $PC=PA+PB$  by Ptolemy. Let  $x=AB=BC=CA$ . $P$  is the Fermat Point of  $\triangle DEF$ , so we have  $$ \triangle DEF = \triangle PEF + \triangle PDE + \triangle PDF = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{4} (PD \cdot PE + PE \cdot PF + PF \cdot PD) $$  Now one can calculate by using Sine Law & Area arguments in  $\triangle PDC, \triangle PEC$  that   $$ PD=PC \cdot \frac{DB}{BC}=PC \cdot \frac{DB}{x}, \text{       }PE = PC \cdot \frac{AE}{AC} = PC \cdot \frac{AE}{x} \text{ 
     },PF=\frac{PA \cdot PB}{PC} $$  Now, also by using sine law in  $\triangle DAC, \triangle EBC$ , we can easily get  $DC=x \cdot \frac{PC}{PA}$  and  $CE=x \cdot \frac{PC}{PB}$ .

This implies  $\frac{DB}{x} = \frac{DC-x}{x} = \frac{PC}{PA}-1 = \frac{PB}{PA}$ . Also, this shows  $\frac{AE}{x} = \frac{CE-x}{x} = \frac{PC}{PB}-1=\frac{PA}{PB}$ .

Now we calculate.   $$ \triangle DEF = \triangle PEF + \triangle PDE + \triangle PDF = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{4} (PD \cdot PE + PE \cdot PF + PF \cdot PD) $$     $$ = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{4} (PC^2 \cdot \frac{DB}{x} \cdot \frac{AE}{x} + PA \cdot PB \cdot (\frac{DB+AE}{x}) )  $$     $$ =\frac{\sqrt{3}}{4} ( PC^2 \cdot \frac{PB}{PA} \cdot \frac{PA}{PB} + PA \cdot PB \cdot (\frac{PB}{PA}+\frac{PA}{PB})) $$     $$ =\frac{\sqrt{3}}{4} ( PC^2 + PA^2 + PB^2 )  = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} ( PA^2+PB^2+PA \cdot PB ) = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} x^2 = 2 \triangle ABC $$

Throughout the proof  **signed area** will be used.**[b]Lemma:**[/b]Let  $ABC$  be an equilateral triangle, and point  $P$  on its circumcircle. Let  $PB$  and  $AC$  intersect at  $E$ , and  $PC$  and  $AB$  intersect at  $F$ .Then  $ {[EPF]}={[ABPC]}$ **Proof:**  Let the tangent to  $(ABC)$  at  $A$  meet  $BP$  at  $J$  .Then applying pascal's theorem on hexagon  $AACPBB$  we get  $JF \parallel BB \parallel AC$   . So   $$ {[EPF]}={[ECF]}-{[ECP]}={[ECJ]}-{[ECP]}={[PCJ]}={[PCB]}+{[BCJ]}={[PCB]}+{[BCA]}={[BPCA]}={[ABPC]} $$  .
 **Problem :** 
