Assume that  $P$  lies on the small arc  $AB$ . Let the triangle have side  $s$  and set  $PA = a$ ,  $PB = b$ ,  $PC = c$  for signed numbers  $a, b > 0$  and  $c < 0$ .

Then the area of the triangle  $\tfrac{\sqrt{3}}{4} s^2$  also equals
\[[PBC] + [PAC] - [PAB] = -\frac{\sqrt{3}}{4}(ab + bc + ca).\]
Then
\[ab + bc + ca = -s^2.\]

By shooting lemma  $AP \cdot AD = AB^2 = AC^2 = s^2$  so  $AD = s^2/a$  and
\[PD = AD - AP = \frac{s^2 - a^2}{a}.\]
Similarly we find  $PE$  and  $PF$ . Now we are ready to finish:
\begin{align*}
[DEF] & = [PEF] + [PFD] + [PDE]
& = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{4} \left(\frac{(s^2 - b^2)(s^2 - c^2)}{bc} + \frac{(s^2 - c^2)(s^2 - a^2)}{ca} + \frac{(s^2 - a^2)(s^2 - b^2)}{ab}\right)
& = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{4abc} \big(a(s^2 - b^2)(s^2 - c^2) + b(s^2 - c^2)(s^2 - a^2) + c(s^2 - a^2)(s^2 - b^2) \big)
& = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{4abc} \big(s^4(a + b + c) - s^2(a^2b + a^2c + b^2a + b^2c + c^2a + c^2b) + abc(ab + bc + ca)\big)
& = -\frac{s^2 \sqrt{3}}{4abc} (a^2b + a^2c + b^2a + b^2c + c^2a + c^2b + abc)
& = -\frac{s^2 \sqrt{3}}{4abc} \big((a + b + c)(ab + bc + ca) - 2abc \big)
& = 2[ABC]
\end{align*}
where we note  $a + b + c = 0$  by Ptolemy.

My first bary-bash problem! As per the usual, let  $A = (1:0:0), B = (0:1:0), C = (0:0:1)$ . Let  $P = (a:b:c)$ . Clearly, as  $ACPB$  is cyclic,  $$ ab + ac + bc = 0. $$  We also know that  $P,C,F$  are collinear. Let  $P: (a,b,c)$  and  $F: (x,y,z)$ . Using EGMO Theorem 7.2, since,  $P,C,F$  are collinear:  $$ bx-ay = 0 \Longleftrightarrow bx =ay. $$  Since  $A,B,F$  are collinear by EGMO Theorem 7.2:  $$ -z = 0 \Longleftrightarrow z = 0. $$  It follows that  $F: \left(\frac{a}{a+b}:\frac{b}{a+b}:0 \right)$  and by symmetry,  $D: \left(0,\frac{b}{b+c},\frac{c}{b+c} \right)$ ,  $E: \left(\frac{a}{a+c}:0:\frac{c}{a+c} \right)$ . The area of  $\frac{[DEF]}{[ABC]}$  is by EGMO Theorem 7.1:
\begin{align*} \begin{vmatrix} 0 & \frac{b}{b+c} & \frac{c}{b+c}  \frac{a}{a+c} & 0 & \frac{c}{a+c}  \frac{a}{a+b} & \frac{b}{a+b} & 0 \end{vmatrix} =  \frac{1}{(a+b)(b+c)(a+c)}\begin{vmatrix} 0 & b & c  a & 0 & c  a & b & 0 \end{vmatrix} .\end{align*}
We know that   $$ \frac{1}{(a+b)(b+c)(c+a)} = \frac{1}{-abc + (a+b+c)(ab+bc+ca)} = \frac{1}{-abc}. $$  Additionally, \begin{align*} \begin{vmatrix} 0 & b & c  a & 0 & c  a & b & 0 \end{vmatrix} = 0 \begin{vmatrix} 0 & c  b & 0 \end{vmatrix} - b  \begin{vmatrix} a & c  a & 0 \end{vmatrix} + c \begin{vmatrix} a & 0  a & b \end{vmatrix} = 2abc. \end{align*}
It follows that  $\frac{| [DEF] |}{ | [ABC] | }=2$  as desired.

