Set  $ABC$  as the reference triangle with side length  $1$ .
Let  $P=(u:v:w)$  with  $uv+vw+wu=0$  as  $P$  lies on  $(ABC)$ . $D$  lies on the cevian from  $A$  to  $P$  so it is of the form  $(t:v:w)$ . But  $D$  also lies on  $BC$  so its  $x$ -coordinate is  $0$ . $\therefore D=(0:v:w)=\left(0,\frac{v}{v+w}, \frac{w}{v+w}\right)$ .
Similarly,  $E=(u:0:w)=\left(\frac{u}{u+w}, 0, \frac{w}{u+w}\right)$  $F=(u:v:0)=\left(\frac{u}{u+v}, \frac{v}{u+v}, 0\right)$ Therefore, 
\begin{align*}
\frac{[DEF]}{[ABC]} &= \frac{1}{(u+v)(v+w)(w+u)} \begin{vmatrix} 0 & v & w  u & 0 & w  u & v & 0 \end{vmatrix}
&= \frac{0\begin{vmatrix} 0 & w  v & 0 \end{vmatrix}+v\begin{vmatrix} w & u 0 & u \end{vmatrix}+w\begin{vmatrix} u & 0  u & v \end{vmatrix}}{(u+v)(v+w)(w+u)} 
&= \frac{v(wu-0u)+w(uv-0u)}{(u+v)(v+w)(w+u)}  
&= \frac{2uvw}{(u+v)(v+w)(w+u)} 
&= \frac{2uvw}{\tfrac{-uv}{w}\tfrac{-vw}{u}\tfrac{-wu}{v}}  
&= \frac{2uvw}{-uvw}
&= -2
\end{align*}
The third last line follows from $uv+vw+wu=0\implies uv+w(u+v)=0\implies u+v=\frac{-uv}{w}$  with a similar method for other cases. $\frac{[DEF]}{[ABC]}=-2$  is enough to imply that the area of  $\triangle DEF$  is twice the area of  $\triangle ABC$ , as desired.

Use bary wrt  $\triangle ABC$ . Then let  $P=(q:r:t)$  s.t.  $qr+qt+rt=0$  since it lies on the circumcircle. We can now find   $$ D=\left(0, \frac{r}{r+t}, \frac{t}{r+t}\right),E=\left(\frac{q}{q+t}, 0, \frac{t}{q+t}\right),F=\left(\frac{q}{q+r}, \frac{r}{q+r},0 \right). $$   Then the desired ratio is simply   $$ \left|\begin{matrix} 
