\end{align*}
Now we write  $x^2+y^2$  in the form  $m(x+y)^2+n(x-y)^2$  so we can put in  $a,b$ . Simple algebra gets  $x^2+y^2=\frac{(x+y)^2+(x-y)^2}{2}$ , so we have:
