The problem is worded quite strangely, so in fact I will rephrase it as such (clearly it is an equivalent statement):
<blockquote>Let  $\omega$  be a circle, and let  $P_1,P_2,\ldots, P_{2n}$  be  $2n$  distinct points on  $\omega$ . The coloring step is the same. Show that for any point  $Q$  which is not equal to  $P_i$  for any  $1 \leq i \leq 2n$ , the number of counterclockwise arcs of the form  $R_i \to B_i$  that contain  $Q$  does not depend on the choice of the ordering of the red points.</blockquote>
The key claim is as follows:**Claim:** Suppose one swaps  $R_i$  and  $R_{i+1}$ , where  $1 \leq i < n$  (call this a swap). Then the number of arcs of the given form does not change for any  $Q$ .**Proof:** All of the red points other than  $R_i$  and  $R_{i+1}$  can be ignored, since they don't change anything. All of the blue points which are paired up with  $R_1,R_2,\ldots,R_{i-1}$  can also be ignored. After doing this, suppose that going counterclockwise from  $R_i$ , there are  $a$  (nonpaired) blue points, then  $R_{i+1}$ , then  $b$  nonpaired blue points, and then  $R_i$  again. Now consider the following cases:**Case 1** -  $a,b>0$ . Then let  $X$  be the first of the  $a$  blue points after  $R_i$  and  $Y$  be the first of the  $b$  blue points after  $R_{i+1}$ . Clearly, before the swap,  $B_i=X$  and  $B_{i+1}=Y$ . After the swap, clearly  $B_i=Y$  and  $B_{i+1}=X$ . Therefore, the set of arcs of the form  $R \to B$  doesn't actually change, so clearly the number of arcs that contain  $Q$  doesn't change either.**Case 2** -  $a=0$  (and therefore  $b\geq 2$ ). Let  $X,Y$  be the first and second blue points after  $R_{i+1}$ . Clearly, before the swap,  $B_i=X$  and  $B_{i+1}=Y$ . After the swap,  $B_i=X$  and  $B_{i+1}=Y$ . A diagram displaying the initial configuration is shown below (the other red points and the already-paired blue points are not shown; also credit to v_Enhance because I kind of stole a lot of the code he wrote for his diagram):
