Observe that  $\angle ADO = \angle HAD + \angle MOD$  and  $\angle HAN = \angle HAD + \angle DAN$ . Therefore it suffices to prove  $\angle MOD = \angle DAN$ .**Lemma:** The circumcircle of  $\triangle ABC$  is the reflection of the circumcircle of  $\triangle BHC$  over  $BC$ .
<details><summary>Proof:</summary>Define  $H_A$  and  $Q$  as the intersections of the  $A$ -altitude with  $BC$  and  $\odot{O}$ , respectively. Note that  $\angle CBQ = \angle CAQ$ . If we draw the foot of the  $B$ -altitude, denoted by  $H_B$ , we see that  $\triangle BCH_B$  and  $\triangle ACH_A$  are both right triangles that include  $\angle C$ . Thus \[\angle CBH = \angle CAH_A = \angle CAQ = \angle CBQ.\] Since  $AQ \perp BC$ , we know that  $H$  and  $Q$  are reflections of each other over  $BC$ . But then  $\triangle BHC$  and  $\triangle BQC$  are also reflections of each other, and same for their circumcircles. Since  $A,B,Q,C$  are cyclic the result follows.  $\square$</details>

So, by the lemma,  $P$  and  $N$  are reflections of each other over  $BC$ . In particular,  $MP = MN = PN/2$ . Now note that \[\frac{DM}{DP} = \frac{PR}{PO} = \cos{DPM} \rightarrow \frac{PD}{PO}=\frac{PM}{PR}.\] But note that \[\frac{PM}{PN} = \frac{PR}{PA} = \frac{1}{2} \rightarrow \frac{PD}{PO} = \frac{PM}{PR} = \frac{PN}{PA}.\]

Therefore by ASA Similarity  $\triangle ANP \sim \triangle ODP$ , and so  $\angle MOD = \angle DAN$ .  $\square$ Short, sweet, and simple. The spiral similarity solution is probably a little faster than this one, though, and I never like to use ASA :P

