Let  $F$  be the arc midpoint of  $\overline{BC}$ ; it suffices to show that  $DANO$  is cyclic, or  $FD \cdot FA = FO \cdot FN$ . Now, we proceed with a terrible finish. Observe that \begin{align*} FD \cdot FA &= FD^2+FD\cdot AD &= \frac{(BD\cdot CD)^2}{AD^2} + BD \cdot CD &= \frac{\left(\frac{ac}{b+c} \cdot \frac{ab}{b+c}\right)^2}{bc-\frac{a^2bc}{(b+c)^2}} + \frac{ac}{b+c} \cdot \frac{ab}{b+c}  &= \frac 1{(b+c)^2}\left(\frac{(a^2bc)^2}{bc(b+c)^2-a^2bc} + a^2bc\right)  &= \frac{a^2b^2c^2(b+c)^2}{bc(b+c)^2[(b+c)^2-a^2]} &= \frac{a^2bc}{(b+c)^2-a^2}.\end{align*}
Also, \begin{align*} FM \cdot FO &= FM^2+FM \cdot MO  &=\left(\frac 12 a \tan \frac A2\right)\left(\frac 12 a \tan \frac A2+\frac 12 a \tan(90^\circ-A)\right)  &= \frac 14 a^2\left(\tan^2 \frac A2+\tan(90^\circ-A)\tan \frac A2\right)  &= \frac 14a^2\left(\frac{(1-\cos A)^2}{\sin^2 A} + \frac{\cos A(1-\cos A)}{\sin^2 A}\right)& = \frac{a^2(1-\cos A)}{4(1-\cos A)(1+\cos A)}  &= \frac{a^2}{4(1+\cos A)}  & = \frac 12 \cdot \frac{a^2bc}{(b+c)^2-a^2}. \end{align*} As  $FN=2FM$ , this finishes the problem.

Let  $K$  denote the midpoint of arc  $BC$  not containing  $A,$  and let  $L$  denote the midpoint of arc  $BC$  containing  $A.$  

It is well-known that the radii of  $(ABC)$  and  $(BHC)$  are equal, so we know that  $N$  and  $K$  are reflections of each other over  $\overline{BC}.$  Therefore, it results that  $KO$  is half of  $KL$  and  $KN$  is double  $KM.$  Note that
\begin{align*}
    \angle{DAL} &= \angle{KAL} 
    &= 90^\circ,
\end{align*}
because  $\overline{KL}$  is a diameter of  $(ABC).$  Also, we have  $\angle{DML}=90^\circ,$  so it results that
\begin{align*}
    \angle{DAL}+\angle{DML} &= 90^\circ + 90^\circ 
    &= 180^\circ.
\end{align*}
Hence,  $ADML$  is cyclic, so Power of a Point gives
\begin{align*}
    KD \cdot KA &= KM \cdot KL 
    &= \frac{1}{2}KN \cdot 2KO 
    &= KO \cdot KN,
\end{align*}
from which it follows that  $ADON$  is cyclic. 

Now, it results that
\begin{align*}
    \angle{HAN} &= \angle{ANL} 
    &= 180^\circ - \angle{ANO} 
    &= \angle{ADO},
\end{align*}
as desired.  $\square$

