Let  $I_A,I_B,I_C$  denote the excenters, let  $E$  denote the intersection of  $(MD)\cap BC$ , let  $X$  be the midpoint of  $BC$ , let  $Q$  be the projection of  $I$  onto  $I_AX$ , and let  $F$  be the reflection of  $I$  in  $I_BI_C$ . We will define  $S$  as  $BC\cap I_BI_C$ , and we will define  $L=(I_ABC)\cap(I_AI_BI_C)$ . It suffices to show that  $S\in MK$  and  $L\in(KID)$  and  $L\in(MAN)$ .

We start with showing that  $S,M,K$  collinear. Firstly, note that by radical axis on  $(I_ABC),(BCI_BI_C),(I_AI_BI_C)$ , we have that  $I_ALS$  collinear. Note that  $I_A,M,D,I,A,F$  are all on the  $A$ -angle bisector. Under  $\sqrt{bc}$  inversion in  $ABC$  at  $A$ , we have that  $BC$  and  $(ABC)$  are swapped, so  $D$  and  $M$  are swapped. We already know that  $I$  and  $I_A$  are swapped, so we have that  $AD\cdot AM=AI\cdot AI_A$ . Note that the radical axis of  $(MD)$  and  $(I_ABC)$  must be perpendicular to the  $A$ -angle bisector (line joining the centers), and since we showed that  $A$  is on it, it must be  $I_BI_C$ . Therefore, by radical axis on  $(MD),(I_ABC),(ABC)$ , we have that  $S,M,K$  collinear.

We now show that  $L\in(KID)$ . Let  $\phi_D$  be the inversion at  $D$  with (negative) power  $DB\cdot DC$ . We see that  $(M,A)$  and  $(I,I_A)$  are swapped by power of a point. Note then that  $\phi_D((MD))$  is a line through  $\phi_D(M)=A$  that is perpendicular to  $DM$ , or  $I_BI_C$ . Also, we see that  $\phi_D((ABC))=(MCB)=(ABC)$ , so  $\phi_D(K)=I_BI_C\cap(ABC)=X$ . We claim that  $\phi_D(L)=Q$ .

Note that  $\phi_D(E)=\phi_D((MD))\cap\phi_D(BC)=I_BI_C\cap BC=S$ . Therefore,  $\phi_D(L)=\phi_D(I_AS)\cap\phi_D(I_ABC)=(DIE)\cap(I_ABC)$ , so it suffices to show that  $EDIQ$  cyclic. We showed above that  $SD\cdot SE=SL\cdot SI_A$  since  $S$  is on the radical axis of  $(I_ABC)$  and  $(MD)$ . Therefore, if we were able to show that  $S,I,Q$  were colliner, we would be done by power of a point. Actually this is well known, but we reproduce the proof here. Let  $\phi_I$  be the inversion at  $I$  with power  $IA\cdot II_A$ . Note that  $\phi_I$  swaps  $A,B,C$  with their corresponding excenters, and  $\phi_I(L)=\phi_I((I_ABC)\cap(I_AI_BI_C))=I_BI_C\cap(ABC)=X$ . Therefore,  $\phi_I(S)=\phi_I(I_AL)\cap\phi_I(I_BI_C)=(XQI)\cap (BIC)$ , so it suffices to show that  $AIQX$  cyclic, but this is true because  $\angle IQX=\angle IAX=\pi/2$ . Therefore, we have shown that  $\phi_D(L)=Q$ .

Thus, since  $I_A,Q,X$  collinear, we have that  $D,\phi_D(I_A),\phi_D(Q),\phi_D(X)=D,I,L,K$  cyclic, so  $L\in(KID)$ . We will now show that  $L\in(MAN)$ .

We will use  $\phi_I$  to do this. Note that  $\phi_I(M)=\phi_I((ABC))\cap\phi_I(IA)=(I_AI_BI_C)\cap IA=F$ ,  $\phi_I(A)=I_A$ ,  $\phi_I(N)=2\phi_I(M)-I=2Q-I$ , and  $\phi_I(L)=X$ . Thus, it suffices to show that  $F,I_A,X,2Q-I$  cyclic. But  $\angle I_A(2Q-I)X=\angle I_AIX$  (since  $Q$  is the foot from  $I$  to  $I_AX$ ), and  $\angle I_AIX=\pi-\angle XIA=\pi-\angle I_AFX$ , so  $\angle I_A(2Q-I)X+\angle I_AFX=\pi$ , so the desired points are cyclic, so  $L\in(MAN)$ , completing the proof.  $\blacksquare$

