Here's my long but elementary solution:
Let  $I_A$  be the  $A-excenter$  of  $\triangle{ABC}$ ,  $Z = SI \cup \odot{(BIC)}$ , and  $L = M'I \cup SI_A$ . Let the circle with diameter  $\overline{DM}$  cuts  $\overline{BC}$  again at  $T$ , and finally we define  $M'$  as antipode of  $M$ . It's not hard to see, that  $M'$ ,  $D$ , and  $K$  are collinear, since  $\angle{DKM}=90^{\circ} = \angle{M'KM}$ .
<span style="color:#f00">**Claim.**</span> **$M'$  lies on  $SA$ .**
<details><summary>Proof.</summary>Note that   $$ \measuredangle{DTM'} = \measuredangle{BTM'} = 90^{\circ} = \measuredangle{M'AM} = \measuredangle{DAM'} $$   hence  $ADTM'$  is cyclic. Now, applying radical axis theorem on  $\Omega$ ,  $\odot{(DKMT)}$ , and  $\odot{(ADTM')}$  we have  $M'$  lies on  $SA$ .  $\square$</details>
<span style="color:#f00">**Claim.**</span> **$Z$  lies on  $M'I_A$ . Furthemore,  $I$  is the orthocenter of  $\triangle{M'SI_A}$ .**
<details><summary>Proof.</summary>By PoP we have   $$ SI \times SZ = SB \times SC = SA \times SM' $$   hence  $I$ .  $Z$ ,  $M'$ ,  $A$  are concyclic and   $$ \measuredangle{IZM'}=90^{\circ} $$   On the other hand   $$ \measuredangle{IZI_A}=90^{\circ} $$   since  $II_A$  is the diameter of  $\odot{BIC}$  by Fact 5. Hence  $\measuredangle{IZM'}=\measuredangle{IZI_A}$  means  $Z \in \overline{M'I_A}$ . From  $\overline{SZ} \perp \overline{M'I_A}$  and  $\overline{I_{A}A} \perp \overline{SM'}$  we immediately get  $I$  is the orthocenter of  $\triangle{M'SI_A}$ .  $\square$</details>
<span style="color:#f00">**Claim.**</span> **$L$  lies on  $\odot{(BIC)}$ .**
<details><summary>Proof.</summary>From last claim we have  $\overline{M'L} \perp \overline{SI_{A}}$ , thus  $\angle{ILI_A}=\angle{M'LI_A}=90^{\circ}=\angle{IZI_A}$ .  $\square$</details>
<span style="color:#f00">**Claim.**</span> **$L$ ,  $K$ ,  $I$ ,  $D$  are concyclic.**
<details><summary>Proof.</summary>We start by following:  $\measuredangle{SZM'}=90^{\circ} = \measuredangle{SKM'}$  and  $\measuredangle{SLM'} = 90^{\circ} = \measuredangle{STM'}$ , means that  $SLKTZM'$  is cyclic. Now, let's angle chase:   $$ \measuredangle{LID} = \measuredangle{LII_A} = \measuredangle{LZI_A} = \measuredangle{LZM'} = \measuredangle{LKM'} = \measuredangle{LKD} $$   which proves the claim.</details>
<span style="color:#f00">**Claim.**</span> **$MANL$  is cyclic.**
<details><summary>Proof.</summary>It's well-known from properties of orthocenter that  $A$ ,  $S$ ,  $L$ , and  $I$  lie on a circle with diameter  $\overline{SI}$ . Therefore   $$ \measuredangle{ANL} = 2 \measuredangle{ASL} = 2 \measuredangle{AIL} = 2 \measuredangle{I_{A}IL} = \measuredangle{I_{A}ML} $$   where the last equality from Fact 5.  $\square$</details>
From all above claims we conclude that  $L=L_1$  or  $L=L_2$  and  $\overline{MN}$  is the perpendicular bisector of  $\overline{IL}$ . Let  $X = \overline{MN} \cup \overline{IL}$ . Finally   $$ \measuredangle{M'XM} = \measuredangle{I'XM} = 90^{\circ} $$   means that  $X$  lies on a circle with diameter  $\overline{M'M}$ , which is just  $\Omega$ .  $\blacksquare$

