We will start by proving the following useful lemma.

<span style="color:red">**Lemma:**</span> Let the internal angle bisector of  $\triangle ABC$  meet side  $BC$  at  $D$ . Ray  $ AD$  intersects the circumcircle  $ \Omega$  of  $\triangle ABC$  at  $ E$ . The circle  $ \omega$  with diameter  $ DE$  meets  $ \Omega$  at  $ F\ne E$ . Then  $ AF$  is a symmedian of  $ \triangle ABC$ . [(Russia 2009)](https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c6h276014_symmedian_line)

*Proof.* Let  $K$  be the symmedian point,  $I$  be the incenter, and  $M$  be the midpoint of  $BC$ . It suffices to show that  $A,K,F$  are colinear. We will do so with phantom points.

First, note that  $M$  lies on  $\omega$  since  $\measuredangle DME=90^\circ$  as  $M,E$  lie on the perpendicular bisector of  $BC$ . Define  $F'=AK\cap\omega$ . We will show that  $F'DME$  is cyclic, implying  $F'=F$  which finishes the problem.

We now employ Barycentric Coordinates. Set  $A=(1,0,0),B=(0,1,0),C=(0,0,1)$ . Since  $F'$  is on the  $A$ -symmedian, it can be parameterized by  $F'=(t:b^2:c^2)$  but it is also on  $\Omega$  so  $\text{Pow}_\Omega (F')=0$ . Of course,  $\Omega$  has equation  $a^2yz+b^2zx+c^2xy=0$ , so plugging in  $F'$  gives  $a^2b^2c^2+2tb^2c^2=0 \Rightarrow t=-\frac{a^2}2$ . So  $$ F'=\left(-\frac{a^2}2,b^2,c^2\right)=(-a^2:2b^2:2c^2). $$  Of course,  $M=(0:1:1)$ . By the angle bisector theorem,\begin{align*}D&=(0:b:c)I&=(a:b:c)=\left(\frac{a}{a+b+c},\frac{b}{a+b+c},\frac{c}{a+b+c}\right)I_a&=(-a:b:c)=\left(\frac{-a}{-a+b+c},\frac{b}{-a+b+c},\frac{c}{-a+b+c}\right).\end{align*}By Fact 5,  $E$  is the midpoint of  $II_a$  so  $E=\frac{I+I_a}2=\frac12\cdot\frac{1}{(a+b+c)(-a+b+c)}[(-a^2+ab+ac,-ab+b^2+bc,-ac+bc+c^2)+(-a^2-ab-ac,ab+b^2+bc,ac+bc+c^2)].$  Thus,  $$ E=(-a^2:b^2+bc:c^2+bc). $$   Plugging in  $D,M,E$  into the general circle equation,  $-a^2yz-b^2zx-c^2xy+(ux+vy+wz)(x+y+z)$ , gives $M:-a^2+2(v+w)=0$  $D:-a^2bc+(b+c)(bv+cw)=0$  $E:-a^2bc(b+c)^2+a^2b^2(c^2+bc)+a^2c^2(b^2+bc)+(-a^2u+(b^2+bc)v+(c^2+bc)w)(-a^2+(b+c)^2)=0$ We can solve the first two equations with substitution (or Cramer's Rule) to get  $v=\frac{a^2c}{2(b+c)}$  and  $w=\frac{a^2b}{2(b+c)}$ . Substituting these values into the third equation, we get

\begin{align*}-a^2bc(b+c)^2+a^2bc(b+c)^2+(-a^2u+a^2bc)(-a^2+(b+c)^2)&=0
a^2(-u+bc)(-a^2+(b+c)^2)&=0
u&=bc.\end{align*}
Hence,  $\omega$  has equation  $$ -a^2yz-b^2zx-c^2xy+[(bc)x+\left(\frac{a^2c}{2(b+c)}\right)y+\left(\frac{a^2b}{2(b+c)}\right)z](x+y+z)=0. $$  To see if  $F'\in\omega$ , we must check that  $\text{Pow}_\omega (F')=0$  but  $$ \text{Pow}_\omega (F')=-a^2(2b^2)(2c^2)-b^2(2c^2)(-a^2)-c^2(-a^2)(2b^2)+[(bc)(-a^2)+\left(\frac{a^2c}{2(b+c)}\right)(2b^2)+\left(\frac{a^2b}{2(b+c)}\right)(2c^2)](-a^2+2b^2+2c^2)=0\Rightarrow 0+[-a^2bc+a^2bc](-a^2+2b^2+2c^2)=0 $$  which is clearly true. So  $F'=F$ , finishing.  $\square$ Now, back to the main problem. 

Forget all point labels in the lemma and obey the original problem statement. Furthermore, let  $I_A,I_B,I_C$  be the  $A$ -extener, $B$ -excenter, and  $C$ -excenter, respectively, let  $W$  be the midpoint of  $I_BI_C$ , let  $T$  be the intersection of ray  $WI$  with  $\Omega$ , and let  $L$  be the reflection of  $I$  over  $T$ . By construction,  $T$  lies on  $\Omega$ , so it suffices to show that  $L$  is on both  $(KID)$  and  $(MAN)$ . 

[asy] size(14cm); defaultpen(fontsize(10pt)); pen pri=heavyred; pen sec=orange; pen tri=olive; pen qua=purple+pink; pen fil=invisible; pen sfil=invisible; pen tfil=invisible; pen qfil=invisible;

pair O,A,B,C,I,D,M,WW,K,SS,NN,IA,IB,IC,T,L; O=(0,0); A=dir(140); B=dir(210); C=dir(330); I=incenter(A,B,C); D=extension(A,I,B,C); M=dir(270); WW=dir(90); K=2*foot(O,WW,D)-WW; SS=extension(M,K,B,C); NN=(I+SS)/2; IA=2M-I; IB=extension(B,I,IA,C); IC=extension(C,I,IA,B); T=2*foot(O,I,WW)-WW; L=2T-I;

draw(IA--A,qua+Dotted); filldraw(IA--IB--IC--cycle,qfil,qua); draw(IC--SS,qua); filldraw(circumcircle(M,A,NN),tfil,tri); filldraw(circumcircle(K,I,D),tfil,tri); draw(I--SS,tri+dashed); draw(WW--L,tri);filldraw(circle(O,1),fil,pri); filldraw(A--B--C--cycle,fil,pri); draw(B--SS,pri);

dot("\(A\)",A,dir(110)); dot("\(B\)",B,dir(215)); dot("\(C\)",C,dir(-10)); dot("\(I\)",I,N); dot("\(D\)",D,SE); dot("\(M\)",M,SE); dot("\(W\)",WW,N); dot("\(K\)",K,dir(285)); dot("\(S\)",SS,W); dot("\(N\)",NN,NW); dot("\(I_A\)",IA,S); dot("\(I_B\)",IB,NE); dot("\(I_C\)",IC,NW); dot("\(T\)",T,dir(195)); dot("\(L\)",L,dir(240)); [/asy]

We proceed by cranking out the coordinates of all labeled points. The following list of points are immediate: \begin{align*}A&=(1:0:0) B&=(0:1:0) C&= (0:0:1) I&=(a:b:c) D&=(0:b:c) M&=(-a^2:b^2+bc:c^2+bc) K&=(-a^2:2b^2:2c^2) I_A&=(-a:b:c) I_C&=(a:b:-c)W&=(a^2:-b^2+bc:-c^2+bc)\end{align*} where we computed the coordinates of  $W=\frac{I_B+I_C}2$  using the midpoint formula on normalized  $I_B,I_C$ . All other points were computed in the starting lemma.

Now, note that  $T=WI\cap \Omega\ne W$ . The equation for the line passing through points  $W$  and  $I$  is   $$ \begin{vmatrix}x&y&z a^2&-b^2+bc&-c^2+bc a&b&c\end{vmatrix}=0 \Longrightarrow (2bc^2-2b^2c)x+(abc-a^2c-ac^2)y+(-abc+a^2b+ab^2)z=0 $$   and the equation for the circumcircle of  $\triangle ABC$  is   $$ a^2yz+b^2zx+c^2xy=0. $$   Hence, the coordinates of  $T$  is one of the solutions to the system \begin{align*}(2bc^2-2b^2c)x+(abc-a^2c-ac^2)y+(-abc+a^2b+ab^2)z&=0 a^2yz+b^2zx+c^2xy&=0 x+y+z&=1\end{align*} which has the solutions  $(a^2:-b^2+bc:-c^2+bc)$  and  $(-2a(a+b-c)(a-b+c) : 4b^2(a+b-c): 4c^2(a-b+c))$ . Since  $W=(a^2:-b^2+bc:-c^2+bc)$ , pick   $$ T=(-2a(a+b-c)(a-b+c) : 4b^2(a+b-c): 4c^2(a-b+c)). $$  Now, note that  $\frac{L+I}2=T \Rightarrow L=2T-I$  where the coordinates of  $L$  and  $I$  are normalized. Hence, with some computation, we find \begin{align*}L&=2\left(\frac{-2a(a+b-c)(a-b+c)}{2 (a + b + c) (-a^2 + a b + a c + 2 b^2 - 4 b c + 2 c^2)},\frac{4b^2(a+b-c)}{2 (a + b + c) (-a^2 + a b + a c + 2 b^2 - 4 b c + 2 c^2)},\frac{4c^2(a-b+c)}{2 (a + b + c) (-a^2 + a b + a c + 2 b^2 - 4 b c + 2 c^2)}\right)-\left(\frac{a}{a+b+c},\frac{b}{a+b+c},\frac{c}{a+b+c}\right)&=\left((-a^2 : b(a+2b-2c) : c(a+2c-2b))\right).\end{align*}

To find the coordinates of  $S$ , note that it can be parameterized by  $(0:t:1-t)$  since it is on  $BC$ . But since  $S,K,M$  are colinear, we must have   $$ \begin{vmatrix}0&t&1-t -a^2&2b^2&2c^2 -a^2&b^2+bc&c^2+bc\end{vmatrix}=0\Rightarrow t=\frac{b}{b-c} $$   so   $$ S=\left(0:\frac{b}{b-c}:\frac{-c}{b-c}\right). $$   Finally, we can compute the coordinates of  $N$  with the midpoint formula:   $$ N=\frac{S+I}2=\frac{\left(0,\frac{b}{b-c},\frac{-c}{b-c}\right)+\left(\frac{a}{a+b+c},\frac{b}{a+b+c},\frac{c}{a+b+c}\right)}2=(ac-ab:-ab-2b^2:ac+2c^2)). $$  At this point, recall  $T$  is clearly on  $\Omega$  by construction. Hence, to finish, it suffices to check that  $L$  lies on the circumcircles of  $\triangle KID$  and  $\triangle MAN$ . 

Recall that the general barycentric circle equation is  $-a^2yz-b^2zx-c^2xy+(ux+vy+wz)(x+y+z)=0$  for constants  $u,v,w$ . 

Plugging in  $K,I,D$  into the general circle equation gives
\begin{align*}&\bullet K:-a^2(2b^2)(2c^2)-b^2(2c^2)(-a^2)-c^2(-a^2)(2b^2)+(u(-a^2)+v(2b^2)+w(2c^2))(-a^2+2b^2+2c^2)=0 \Longrightarrow u(-a^2)+v(2b^2)+w(2c^2)=0
&\bullet I:-a^2bc-b^2ca-c^2ab+(ua+vb+wc)(a+b+c)=0\Longrightarrow au + bv + cw = abc
&\bullet D:-a^2bc-b^2(c)(0)-c^2(0)(b)+(u(0)+vb+wc)(0+b+c)=0 \Longrightarrow bv + cw = \frac{a^2bc}{b+c}
\end{align*} 

Substituting  $D$  into  $I$  gives   $$ au+\frac{a^2bc}{b+c} = abc \Longrightarrow u=\left(bc-\frac{abc}{b+c}\right)=\frac{2bc(b+c-a)(b-c)}{2(b^2-c^2)}. $$   Now we have a two variable system of equations which we can solve to get   $$ v=\frac{-a^2c(a-b+c)}{2(b^2-c^2)} $$   and   $$ w=\frac{a^2b(a+b-c)}{2(b^2-c^2)}. $$   Hence  $(KID)$  has equation   $$ (KID):-a^2yz-b^2zx-c^2xy+\left[\left(\frac{2bc(b+c-a)(b-c)}{2(b^2-c^2)}\right)x+\left(\frac{-a^2c(a-b+c)}{2(b^2-c^2)}\right)y+\left(\frac{a^2b(a+b-c)}{2(b^2-c^2)}\right)z\right](x+y+z)=0. $$  Plugging in  $M,A,N$  into the general circle equation gives 
\begin{align*}
&\bullet M:-a^2(b^2+bc)(c^2+bc)-b^2(c^2+bc)(-a^2)-c^2(-a^2)(b^2+bc)+(u(-a^2)+v(2b^2)+w(2c^2))(-a^2+2b^2+2c^2)=0 \Longrightarrow 0+(-a^2u+(b+c)bv+(b+c)cw)=0
&\bullet A:-a^2(0)(0)-b^2(0)(1)-c^2(1)(0)+(u(1)+v(0)+w(0))(1+0+0)=0\Longrightarrow u=0
&\bullet N:-a^2(-ab-2b^2)(ac+2c^2)-b^2(ac+2c^2)(ac-ab)-c^2(ac-ab)(-ab-2b^2)+(u(ac-ab)+v(-ab-2b^2)+w(ac+2c^2))((ac-ab)+(-ab-2b^2)+(ac+2c^2))=0 \Longrightarrow a^2bc(a+2b)(a+2c)-ab^2(c-b)(a+2c)+ac^2b(c-b)(a+2b)+(au(c-b)-bv(a+2b)+cw(a+2c))(2ac-2ab+2c^2-2b^2)=0.\end{align*} 

Since  $u=0$  from  $A$ , we can reduce this to a two variable system of equations which we can solve to get   $$ v=\frac{-a^2c}{4(b-c)} $$   and   $$ w=\frac{a^2b}{4(b-c)}. $$   Hence  $(MAN)$  has equation   $$ (MAN):-a^2yz-b^2zx-c^2xy+\left[\left(\frac{-a^2c}{4(b-c)}\right)y+\left(\frac{a^2b}{4(b-c)}\right)z\right](x+y+z)=0. $$  Finally, compute 

\begin{align*}\text{Pow}_\text{(KID)}(L)&=-a^2b(a+2b-2c)c(a+2c-2b) -b^2c(a+2c-2b)(-a^2)-c^2(-a^2)b(a+2b-2c)+\left[\left(\frac{2bc(b+c-a)(b-c)}{2(b^2-c^2)}\right)(-a^2)+\left(\frac{-a^2c(a-b+c)}{2(b^2-c^2)}\right)b(a+2b-2c)+\left(\frac{a^2b(a+b-c)}{2(b^2-c^2)}\right)c(a+2c-2b)\right](-a^2+b(a+2b-2c)+c(a+2c-2b))&=-a^2bc(a^2-2b^2-2c^2-ab-ac+bc)+(-1)(a^2bc)\left[\left(\frac{2(b+c-a)(b-c)}{2(b^2-c^2)}\right)+\left(\frac{(a-b+c)}{2(b^2-c^2)}\right)(a+2b-2c)+\left(\frac{-(a+b-c)}{2(b^2-c^2)}\right)(a+2c-2b)\right](-1)(a^2-2b^2-2c^2-ab-ac+bc)&= a^2bc(a^2-2b^2-2c^2-ab-ac+bc)\left[-1+\left(\frac{2(b+c-a)(b-c)}{2(b^2-c^2)}\right)+\left(\frac{(a-b+c)}{2(b^2-c^2)}\right)(a+2b-2c)+\left(\frac{-(a+b-c)}{2(b^2-c^2)}\right)(a+2c-2b)\right]&=a^2bc(a^2-2b^2-2c^2-ab-ac+bc)(2)(b^2-c^2)\left[-2(b^2-c^2)+\left(\frac{2(b+c-a)(b-c)}{1}\right)+\left(\frac{(a-b+c)}{1}\right)(a+2b-2c)+\left(\frac{-(a+b-c)}{1}\right)(a+2c-2b)\right]&=a^2bc(a^2-2b^2-2c^2-ab-ac+bc)(2)(b^2-c^2)\left[-2b^2+2c^2+2(b^2-c^2-ab+ac)+a^2+2ab-2ac-ab-2b^2+2bc+ac+2bc-2c^2-(a^2+2ac-2ab+ab+2bc-2b^2-ac-2c^2+2bc)\right]&=a^2bc(a^2-2b^2-2c^2-ab-ac+bc)(2)(b^2-c^2)\left[0\right]&=0\end{align*}

and

\begin{align*}\text{Pow}_\text{(MAN)}(L)&=-a^2b(a+2b-2c)c(a+2c-2b) -b^2c(a+2c-2b)(-a^2)-c^2(-a^2)b(a+2b-2c)+\left[\left(\frac{-a^2c}{4(b-c)}\right)b(a+2b-2c)+\left(\frac{a^2b}{4(b-c)}\right)c(a+2c-2b)\right](-a^2+b(a+2b-2c)+c(a+2c-2b))&=-a^2bc(a^2-2b^2-2c^2-ab-ac+bc)+a^2bc\left[\left(\frac{-(a+2b-2c)}{4(b-c)}\right)+\left(\frac{a+2c-2b}{4(b-c)}\right)\right](-1)(a^2-2b^2-2c^2-ab-ac+bc)&=-a^2bc(a^2-2b^2-2c^2-ab-ac+bc)\left[1+\left(\frac{-(a+2b-2c)}{4(b-c)}\right)+\left(\frac{a+2c-2b}{4(b-c)}\right)\right]&=-a^2bc(a^2-2b^2-2c^2-ab-ac+bc)\frac{1}{4(b-c)}\left[4(b-c)+\left(\frac{-(a+2b-2c)}{1}\right)+\left(\frac{a+2c-2b}{1}\right)\right]&=-a^2bc(a^2-2b^2-2c^2-ab-ac+bc)\frac{1}{4(b-c)}\left[0\right]&=0.\end{align*}

Since   $$ \text{Pow}_\text{(KID)}(L)=\text{Pow}_\text{(MAN)}(L)=0, $$    $L$  lies on both  $(KID)$  and  $(MAN)$ , completing the proof.  $\blacksquare$

Let  $X$  be the midpoint of  $\widehat{BAC}$ . **Claim:**  $X$ ,  $D$ , and  $K$  are collinear.
*Proof.* Obvious as,   $$ \angle DKM = 90 =  \angle XKM $$  so the claim follows.  $\square$ **Claim:**  $X$ ,  $A$  and  $S$  are collinear.
*Proof.* Note that,
\begin{align*}
-1 = (AK,BC)
\end{align*}
from  $\sqrt{bc}$  inversion. Then projecting at  $M$  we find,
\begin{align*}
-1 = (DS,BC)
\end{align*}
and hence  $S = \overline{XA} \cap \overline{BC}$ .  $\square$ **Claim:**  $DKAS$  is cyclic.
*Proof.* Note that,   $$ \angle MKD = 90 = \angle MAS $$   so the claim follows.  $\square$ Now redefine  $L = \overline{XI} \cap (BIC)$ .**Claim:**  $L$  lies on  $(KID)$ .
*Proof.* It is equivalent to show  $L$  lies on  $(AIS)$  by radical axis, but this is well known.  $\square$ **Claim:**  $L$  lies on  $(MAN)$ .
*Proof.* Note that,
\begin{align*}
\angle LMA = 180 - 2\angle LIM = 180 - 2\angle ASL = 180 - \angle ANL
\end{align*}
so the claim follows. 

However then from properties of mixintillinear touch point  $T$  namely  $M$ ,  $I$ ,  $T$  collinear, the midpoint of  $NL$  is  $T$  so we're done.

Let  $I_A$  be the  $A$ -excenter. Then, by the Incenter-Excenter Lemma,  $B, I, C, I_A$  lie on a circle  $\gamma$  with center  $M$ . 
[asy]
import geometry;

unitsize(1 cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10));

pair A, B, C, D, K, M, N, S;
pair[] I, L;

A = (0.2,2);
B = (0,0);
C = (5,0);
I[0] = incenter(A,B,C);
D = extension(A,I[0],B,C);
M = circumcenter(A,B,C) + reflect(A,I[0])*(circumcenter(A,B,C)) - A;
K = reflect(circumcenter(A,B,C), (D + M)/2)*(M);
S = extension(B,C,K,M);
N = (I[0] + S)/2;
L[1] = intersectionpoints(circumcircle(K,I[0],D), circumcircle(M,A,N))[0];
L[2] = intersectionpoints(circumcircle(K,I[0],D), circumcircle(M,A,N))[1];
I[1] = excenter(B,C,A);

draw(A--I[1], gray(0.7));
draw(Circle((D + M)/2, abs(D - M)/2), gray(0.7));
draw(M--S--I[0], gray(0.7));
draw(I[0]--L[2], gray(0.7));
draw(A--B--C--cycle);
draw(B--S);
draw(circumcircle(A,B,C), red);
draw(circumcircle(K,I[0],D), blue);
draw(circumcircle(M,A,N), heavygreen);
draw(Circle(M, abs(M - I[0])), orange);

dot(" $A$ ", A, NW);
dot(" $B$ ", B, SW);
dot(" $C$ ", C, E);
dot(" $D$ ", D, NE);
dot(" $I$ ", I[0], dir(90));
dot(" $I_A$ ", I[1], SE);
dot(" $K$ ", K, dir(270));
dot(" $L_1$ ", L[1], NW);
dot(" $L_2$ ", L[2], SW);
dot(" $M$ ", M, SE);
dot(" $N$ ", N, SE);
dot(" $S$ ", S, W);
dot((I[0] + L[2])/2);
label(" $\gamma$ ", M + abs(M - I[0])*dir(240), SW);
label(" $\Omega$ ", circumcenter(A,B,C) + circumradius(A,B,C)*dir(60), NE);
[/asy]

Now, invert wrt  $\gamma$ . Note that this inversion swaps  $BC$  and  $\Omega$ , so it swaps  $A$  and  $D$ . Therefore, it takes  $(DMK)$  to the external angle bisector of  $\angle BAC$ . Now, note that  $K'$  lies on  $MK$ , and since  $K$  lies on  $\Omega$ ,  $K'$  must also lie on  $BC$ , so  $K'=S$ . Therefore,  $AS$  is the external angle bisector of  $\angle BAC$ , or  $\angle IAS = 90^\circ$ , so  $A$  lies on the circle with diameter  $IS$ . Next, let  $(SIA)$  intersect  $\gamma$  at  $I$  and  $L$ . Note that the inversion takes  $(KID)$  to  $(SIA)$ , and it fixes  $L$ , so  $L$  lies on  $(KID)$ . Since the center of  $(AIL)$  is  $N$ , we have  $\angle IAL = \frac12 \angle INL$ . Also, we have  $\frac12 \angle INL = \angle MNL$ , so  $\angle MAL = \angle IAL = \angle MNL$ , so  $MANL$  is cyclic. This means that  $L$  is either  $L_1$  or  $L_2$ , so it suffices to prove that the midpoint of  $IL$  lies on  $\Omega$ . Now, let  $X$  be the point diametrically opposite  $M$  on  $\Omega,$  and let  $Y$  be the midpoint of  $BC.$  Since  $\angle MAX = 90^\circ,$  we see that  $X$  lies on  $SA.$  Since  $\angle DKM = \angle DYM = 90^\circ,$  we see that  $DKMY$  is cyclic. Since  $\angle SAD = \angle SKD = 90^\circ,$  we have that  $ADKS$  is cyclic. And since  $\angle SAM = \angle SYM = 90^\circ,$  we have that  $SAYM$  is cyclic. The radical center of  $(DKMY),$   $(ADKS),$  and  $(SAYM)$  is then the point of concurrence of  $SA,$   $KD,$  and  $MY.$  Since  $X$  lies on  $SA$  and  $MY,$  it follows that  $X$  lies on  $KD.$  The radical center of  $(KLID),$   $(AILS),$  and  $(ADKS)$  is then the point of concurrence of  $LI,$   $KD,$  and  $SA.$  Since  $X$  lies on  $SA$  and  $KD,$  it follows that  $X$  lies on  $LI.$  Finally, let  $Z$  be the midpoint of  $LI.$  The perpendicular bisector of  $LI$  is  $MN$ , so  $\angle MZI = 90^\circ$ , and hence,  $Z$  lies on  $\Omega$ .

[asy]
import geometry;

unitsize(1 cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10));

pair A, B, C, D, K, L, M, N, S, X, Y, Z;
pair[] I;

A = (0.2,2);
B = (0,0);
C = (5,0);
I[0] = incenter(A,B,C);
D = extension(A,I[0],B,C);
M = circumcenter(A,B,C) + reflect(A,I[0])*(circumcenter(A,B,C)) - A;
K = reflect(circumcenter(A,B,C), (D + M)/2)*(M);
S = extension(B,C,K,M);
N = (I[0] + S)/2;
I[1] = excenter(B,C,A);
L = reflect(M,N)*(I[0]);
X = 2*circumcenter(A,B,C) - M;
Y = (B + C)/2;
Z = (L + I[0])/2;

draw(A--I[1], gray(0.7));
draw(Circle((D + M)/2, abs(D - M)/2), gray(0.7));
draw(M--S--I[0], gray(0.7));
draw(circumcircle(S,I[0],A), gray(0.7));
draw(S--X--M, gray(0.7));
draw(K--X, gray(0.7));
draw(L--X, gray(0.7));
draw(A--B--C--cycle);
draw(B--S);
