The claim ensures that we will need arbitrarily many labels to label the full lattice, a contradiction.

We claim the answer is all  $c<\sqrt{2}$ .

For the construction, suppose  $c<\sqrt{2}$ . Let  $n$  be any positive integer, and suppose we do the following: For each lattice point  $(x,y)$ , if  $\nu_2(x)=\nu_2(y)=u\le n$ , label it with  $2u+2$ . If  $\nu_2(x)\ne \nu_2(y)$  and  $\nu_2(x+y)\le n$ , label it with  $2\nu_2(x+y)+1$ . Label all other points with  $2n+3$ .**Claim.** This construction works for sufficiently large  $n$ .**Proof.** First, consider any two points labeled  $i$ . If  $i<2n+3$  is odd, then all points  $(x,y)$  labeled  $i$  satisfy  $\min(\nu_2(x),\nu_2(y))=\frac{i-1}{2}$  and  $\nu_2(x)\ne\nu_2(y)$ . Let  $u=\frac{i-1}{2}$ . Then  $(x,y)$  is  $2^u$  times a lattice point  $(x',y')$ , where exactly one of  $x'$  and  $y'$  is odd. The distance between any two such lattice points is at least  $\sqrt{2}$ , so the distance between any two  $(x,y)$  is at least  $\sqrt{2}\cdot2^u=\sqrt{2}^i>c^i$ .

If  $i<2n+3$  is even, then all points  $(x,y)$  labeled  $i$  satisfy  $\nu_2(x)=\nu_2(y)=\frac{i}{2}-1$ . Let  $u=\frac{i}{2}-1$ . Then  $(x,y)$  is  $2^u$  times a lattice point  $(x',y')$ , where  $x'$  and  $y'$  are both odd. The distance between any two such lattice points is at least  $2$ , so the distance between any two  $(x,y)$  is at least  $2\cdot2^u=\sqrt{2}^i>c^i$ .

If  $i=2n+3$ , then notice that for every lattice point  $(x,y)$  labeled  $i$ ,if  $\nu_2(x)=\nu_2(y)=u$ , then  $u>n$ , andif  $\nu_2(x)\ne\nu_2(y)$ , then  $\min(\nu_2(x),\nu_2(y))>n$ .In both cases,  $2^{n+1}\mid x$  and  $2^{n+1}\mid y$ . Thus the distance between any two points labeled  $i$  is at least  $2^{n+1}=\sqrt{2}^{2n+2}$ . Thus choosing  $n$  such that \[\left(\frac{\sqrt{2}}{c}\right)^{2n+2}>c\] suffices.

For the bound, we will show that when  $c=\sqrt{2}$ , such a labeling is impossible, from which the conclusion readily follows.**Claim.** Suppose  $c=\sqrt{2}$ . Then for any nonnegative integer  $n$   any square grid with  $2^n$  lattice points on each sid  must have a lattice point with label greater than or equal to  $2n+1$ .**Proof.** We induct on  $n$ , with the base cases  $n=0$  and  $n=1$  being immediate. Suppose that our claim is true for some  $n-1$ . Then for any  $2^{n-1}\times 2^{n-1}$  grid of lattice points, there exists a point labeled with at least  $2n-1$ . We will prove that for any  $2^n\times 2^n$  grid of lattice points, there exists a point labeled with at least  $2n+1$ .

Assume FTSOC that there exists a valid labeling of a  $2^n\times 2^n$  grid such that no label exceeds  $2n$ . Then every  $2^{n-1}\times 2^{n-1}$  grid has a label of either  $2n-1$  or  $2n$ . Additionally, every pair of  $2n-1$ -labels are at least  $2^{n-1}\sqrt{2}$  apart, and every pair of  $2n$ -labels are at least  $2^n$  apart.

Rather than considering the lattice points as lattice points, consider them as cells in a  $2^n\times 2^n$  grid of unit squares. We will consider all possible locations of the top left corner of a  $2^n\times 2^n$  grid intersecting none of the  $2n-1$ - or  $2n$ -labels. For each  $2n-1$ -label, paint a red  $2^n\times 2^n$  grid with the bottom right corner at the label, and for each  $2n$ -label, paint a blue  $2^n\times 2^n$  grid with the bottom right corner at the label. Notice that a  $2^n\times 2^n$  grid intersects a certain label iff its top left corner lies inside the corresponding red/blue grid. Thus the red and blue grids must cover all possible top left squares, so they cover the whole top left  $(2^{n-1}+1)\times(2^{n-1}+1)$  subgrid. We will prove that this is impossible.

[asy]
            fill((1,1)--(1,5)--(-3,5)--(-3,1)--cycle,red);
