Notice that no two red squares can overlap or share an edge. Otherwise, both the  $x$ -distance and the  $y$ -distance between the bottom right corners of the squares are both  $\le 2^{n-1}$ , and if they are both  $2^{n-1}$ , then they share a vertex, not an edge. Otherwise the distance is less than  $2^{n-1}\sqrt{2}$ , contradiction. Similarly, no two blue squares can overlap or share an edge, but they also cannot share a vertex.

Now we look at the top left  $(2^{n-1}+1)\times (2^{n-1}+1)$  grid (we will henceforth refer to this as the top left grid). The first key observation is that after removing the blue squares from this grid, the resulting polygon is connected. 

[asy]
