[/asy]

To prove this, notice that each large blue square's intersection with the top left grid is a rectangle passing through exactly one of the vertices of the top left grid, with the vertex corresponding to the quadrant in the original grid that the original  $2n$ -label is in.

We now claim that it is possible to find a path from any white square to any other, which proves that the shape is connected. Starting at any white square, move either left or down until neither move is possible. The ending square must have either a border or a blue square both directly to the left of it and directly below it. If they are both blue squares, the blue squares must be part of different rectangles, but then the rectangles would touch at a vertex, contradiction. Thus the ending square must be either the bottommost square of the left edge or the leftmost square of the bottom edge. Traveling along the perimeter of the bottom left rectangle implies that both of these squares are connected (they may be the same square), so one can get from any square to the leftmost square of the bottom edge, which finishes.

