[/asy]

Now that we know the resulting shape is connected, we will prove that it is impossible to cover the rest of the shape with red squares, which finishes. Notice that no single blue square could have covered the entirety of any edge, and two blue squares cannot share an edge, so at least one square from each edge remains. Thus it is impossible to cover the figure with one red square. Therefore, for any red square covering part of the shape, the squares immediately adjacent to it (there must be at least one) cannot be covered by any more red squares. This is a contradiction, so our claim is proven.

Thus, for arbitrarily large  $n$ , there must exist a label  $2n+1$  in a  $2^n\times 2^n$  grid, finishing the proof.

