Finally, I stumbled upon a vertex I hadn't considered yet, which is the key to pointing out a point which  $"\textit{doesn't socialize much}"$  with other vertices; a.k.a. those in the middle. I realized that I was being stupid and I could've repeated the same argument I applied in the first two paragraphs of  $\textcolor{magenta}{\textbf{\text{The Proof.}}}$  to  $v_3$  and win.  $\textcolor{green}{\text{Section 4 End.}}$  **Final rating**: 25 points, converted to 25M in MOHS. May be that I just didn't find the central thread at first, the problem has  $"\text{lots of misleading leads}"$  and  $"\text{strange but valid configurations}"$  and that amplified by my stupidity of not realizing that  $\textbf{the easiest way to compute edge cardinality is by degree counting.}$</details>

I have a different but widely known solution for these kinds of problems 

<details><summary>Solution 1</summary>Now on plugging  $x = y = 0$  in the functional equation we have that 
\begin{align*}
&c = 2c^2 \quad [\text{let } f(0) = c \in \mathbb{R}] 
\implies & c \cdot (c - \frac{1}{2}) = 0 
\end{align*}
Therefore we have  $f(0) = 0$  or  $f(0) = \frac{1}{2} $ .  $\blacksquare$  Now if  $f(0) = 0 $ , we have that   $$  f(x) = 0 \quad \text{for any } x \in \mathbb{R}  $$  Threfore  $f(x)$  is constant for any  $x$  in  $\mathbb{R}$ . $\blacksquare$  Now if  $f(0) = \frac{1}{2}$ , then we have that   $$  f(2y) =  f(y) \quad \text{for any } y \in \mathbb{R}  $$  Which leads us to the following by taking  $y = \frac{t}{2}$ , and as  $y$  is arbitrary in  $\mathbb{R}$  so is  $t$ . Hence we have the formula (which can be checked easily) 
\begin{align*}
f(t) = &f\bigg( \frac{t}{2^n} \bigg) \quad \text{for any } t \in \mathbb{R}
     = &\lim_{n \to \infty} f\bigg( \frac{t}{2^n} \bigg) 
     = &f\bigg( \lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{t}{2^n} \bigg) \quad \text{as } f \text{ is continuous on } \mathbb{R}
     = &f (0) = \frac{1}{2} 	 
\end{align*}
Which completes the proof.</details>

<details><summary>Solution 2</summary>Let's consider the new function  $g : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$  such that it's defined as   $$  g(x) := \ln \circ f(x) + \ln{2} = \ln \circ 2 f(x)  \quad \text{for any } x \in \mathbb{R} $$  But at first we have to show that the map  $g$  is well defined. 

Let's plug in  $x = y = \frac{t}{3}$  for some  $t$   $\in$   $\mathbb{R}$  in the original functional equation. Therefore we have   $$  f(t) = 2 \cdot f\bigg(\frac{t}{3}\bigg)^2 > 0 \quad \text{as } f \neq 0 \in \mathbb{R} $$  Which implies that the composition  $\ln \circ f(x) $  is well defined and hence  $g$  is well defined.

Now the map  $g$  is also a continuous map on  $\mathbb{R}$ . Now our functional equation now becomes   $$  g(x + 2y) = g(x) + g(y) \quad \text{for any } x, y \in \mathbb{R}  $$  Now let's observe that on plugging in  $x = y = 0$  we obtain  $g(0) = 0$ .

Now let's plug in  $x = 0$  in the new functional equation. We obatain that   $$  g(2y) = g(y) \quad \text{for any } y \in \mathbb{R}  $$  Which leads us to the following by taking  $y = \frac{t}{2}$ , and as  $y$  is arbitrary in  $\mathbb{R}$  so is  $t$ . Hence we have the formula (which can be checked easily) 
\begin{align*}
g(t) = &g\bigg( \frac{t}{2^n} \bigg) \quad \text{for any } t \in \mathbb{R}
     = &\lim_{n \to \infty} g\bigg( \frac{t}{2^n} \bigg) 
     = &g\bigg( \lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{t}{2^n} \bigg) \quad \text{as } g \text{ is continuous on } \mathbb{R}
     = &g (0) = 0 	 
\end{align*}
Therefore we have that  $g(x) \equiv 0$  for any  $x$   $\in$   $\mathbb{R}$ . Therefore we have that  $f$  is a constant map on  $\mathbb{R}$  such that 
\begin{align*}
&\ln \circ 2 f(x) = 0 \quad \text{for any } x \in \mathbb{R}  
\implies & 2 f(x) = 1 \quad \text{for any } x \in \mathbb{R}
\implies & f(x) = \frac{1}{2}  \quad \text{for any } x \in \mathbb{R}
\end{align*}
Hence we are done!</details>

