The organizers just took the proposers' solutions and published them as "official". Here is my solution.

<details><summary>Click to expand</summary>We will need  $f$ ,  $\log f$  and  $\log\log f$  to be positive from some point, but this is not ensured. So let us use  $g(x)=f(x)-f(10)+10$  instead of  $f$ . For  $x\ge 10$  we have  $$  g(x)\le C_0 x^2\log x, \quad \log g(x)\le C_1 \log x, \quad \log\log g(x)\le C_2 \log\log x.  $$  (The constants  $C_0,C_1,\ldots$  are positive.)

By Cauchy-Schwarz,  $$ \int_{10}^x \frac1{g'} \cdot \int_{10}^x \frac{g'}{g \cdot \log g} 
\ge \left(\int_{10}^x \frac1{\sqrt{g\cdot \log g}}\right)^2. $$  Here  $$ \int_{10}^x \frac{g'}{g \cdot \log g} = \log\log g(x) - \log\log g(10) < C_2\log\log x $$  and, say for  $x>100$ ,  $$ \int_{10}^x \frac1{\sqrt{g \cdot \log g}}
\ge \int_{10}^x \frac{\mathrm{d}t}{\sqrt{C_0t^2\log t \cdot C_1\log t}}
= C_3\int_{10}^x \frac{\mathrm{d}t}{t\log t}
= C_3(\log\log x - \log\log 10) > C_4\log\log x. $$  Hence,  $$ \int_{10}^x \frac1{f'} = \int_{10}^x \frac1{g'}
\ge \frac{\Big(\int_{10}^{x}\frac1{\sqrt{g\log g}}\Big)^2}{\int_{10}^{x} \frac{g'}{g\log g}}
 > \frac{(C_4\log\log x)^2}{C_2\log\log x} = C_5 \log\log x. $$  * * *

The motivation behind using the function  $\frac{g'}{g\log g}$  was that I wanted to make Cauchy-Schwarz sharp: if  $f(x)\approx x^2\log x$  then we may expect  $f'(x)\approx x\log x$  and  $\log f(x)\approx \log x$ , so in that heuristic case  $\frac1{f'}$  and  $\frac{f'}{f\log f}$  have roughly the same magnitude order.

The solution shows that the bound  $x^2\log x$  can be replaced by  $x^2\log x\log\log x$  (or  $x^2\log x\log\log x\log\log\log x$  and so on). This suggests an easy generalization: if  $f'>0$  is say continuous,  $f>0$  and  $\int_1^\infty \frac{x}{f(x)} \mathrm{d}x = \infty$  then   $\int_1^\infty \frac1{f'} = \infty$ .

* * *

Instead of  $\frac{g'}{g\log g}$ , we may use  $\frac{g'}{g}$ ; that leads to estimates like   $$ \int_a^{a^2} \frac1{g'} \ge \frac{\Big(\int_a^{a^2}\frac1{\sqrt{g}}\Big)^2}{\int_a^{a^2} \frac{g'}{g}}
> C $$  with some constant  $C>0$ . This also implies  $\int_a^{a^2} \frac1{g'}=\infty$ .</details>

