We apply the (relatively) standard trick of completing into an equilateral triangle, from which we can ge the side lengths of the other  $2$  sides, as shown in the diagram. The diameter of the circle should be the shortest altitude from one side to the other side, which we can visually see to be the distance between the side of length  $16$  and the side of length  $10$ . So, the altitude is  $15\sqrt3-8\sqrt3=7\sqrt3$  since the side of the equilateral triangle is  $30$ , hence  $(7\sqrt3)^2=\boxed{147}$ .

Consider the generating function,
\begin{align*}
F(x) = \prod_{k = 1}^{18} (1+x^k)
\end{align*}
Note we are looking for sums of terms that are divisible by  $3$ , which translates to finding the coefficients of all  $x^{3m}$ . Thus we can apply a roots of unity filter. Thus we compute,
\begin{align*}
F(1) &= 2^{18}
F(w) &= \left( 2(1+w)(1+w^2) \right)^6
F(w^2) &= \left( 2(1+w)(1+w^2) \right)^6
\end{align*}
Then the total number of such subsets is,
\begin{align*}
\frac{F(1) + F(w) + F(w^2)}3 &= \frac{2^{18} + 2^{7}(1+w)^6(1+w^2)^6}{3}
&= \frac{2^{18} + 2^7 \cdot (1 + w + w^2 + w^3)^6}{3}
&= \frac{2^{18} + 2^7}{3}
\end{align*}
Then our final probability is,
\begin{align*}
\frac{2^{18} + 2^7}{3 \cdot 2^{18}} = \frac{2^{11} + 1}{3 \cdot 2^{11}} = \frac{683}{2048}
\end{align*}

Consider the generating function:
\[ f(X) = (1+X)(1+X^2)\cdots(1+X^{17})(1+X^{18}). \]
By Roots of Unity Filter, then the number of subsets whose sum is divisible by  $3$  is:
\[ \dfrac{f(1)+f\left(e^{2\pi i/3}\right)+f\left(e^{4\pi i/3}\right)}{3}. \]
Firstly,  $f(1) = 2^{18}$ . Indeed,
\begin{align*}
f\left(e^{2\pi i/3}\right) &= \left(\left(1+e^{2\pi i/3}\right)\left(1+e^{4\pi i/3}\right)\left(1+e^{6\pi i/3}\right)\right)^6 
&= \left(\left(\dfrac{1}{2}+\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}i\right)\cdot \left(\dfrac{1}{2}-\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}i\right)\cdot 2\right)^6=2^6
\end{align*}
In addition,
\begin{align*}
f\left(e^{4\pi i/3}\right) &= \left(\left(1+e^{4\pi i/3}\right)\left(1+e^{8\pi i/3}\right)\left(1+e^{12\pi i/3}\right)\right)^6 
&= \left(\left(1+e^{2\pi i/3}\right)\left(1+e^{4\pi i/3}\right)\left(1+e^{6\pi i/3}\right)\right)^6 = f(e^{2\pi i/3}) = 2^6.
\end{align*}
Thus, it suffices to compute:
\begin{align*}
\dfrac{\left(2^{18}+2^6+2^6\right)/3}{2^{18}} = \dfrac{2^{12}+1+1}{3 \cdot 2^{12}} = \dfrac{4098}{3\cdot 2^{12}} = \dfrac{683}{2^{10}}.
\end{align*}
Thus, the desired answer is  $\boxed{683.}$

