Call a move  $I$  if it ends on the inner circle and  $O$  if it ends on the outer circle. 

Now we will number the points in  $\pmod 5$ . Let  $A=J=0, B=K=1, C=H=2, D=G=3, E=F=4$ . 

An  $I$  move either keeps the number the same or adds it by  $1$ . 

An  $O$  move either keeps the number the same or subtracts it by  $1$ . 

Note that there are the same number of  $I$  moves as  $O$  moves that move from one of the circles to the other (those are the moves that add by  $0$ ). 

The only moves that add or subtract anything are ones from one circle to the same circle. Consider these moves. Since we must end at  $0\pmod 5$ , the number of such  $I$  moves and  $O$  moves are the same  $\pmod 5$ . 

Thus, the difference between the total number of  $I$  moves and  $O$  moves is a multiple of  $5$ . Since the last move must be an  $I$  move, we can either have  $4$ ,  $9$ , or  $14$   $I$  moves excluding the last move. 

So the total is  $\binom{14}{4}+\binom{14}{9}+\binom{14}{14}=1001+2002+1=3\boxed{004}$ .

