Clearly  $\angle I_1AI_2 = \angle A/2$  is fixed so it suffices to minimize  $AI_1 \cdot AI_2$ .

Now note by Law of Sines,
\begin{align*}
AI_1 &= \frac{AB \sin \angle B/2}{\sin \angle AI_1B}
AI_2 &= \frac{AC \sin \angle C/2}{\sin \angle AI_2C}
\end{align*}
so we must maximize the product  $\sin \angle AI_1B \cdot \sin \angle AI_2C$ .

However if we let  $\theta = I_1AB$  then we find that,
\begin{align*}
\angle AI_1B &= 180 - \angle B/2 - \theta
\angle I_2AC &= 180 - \angle C/2 - (\angle A/2 - \theta) = 90 + \angle B/2 + \theta
\end{align*}
Thus we have,
\begin{align*}
\sin \angle AI_1B \cdot \sin \angle AI_2C &= \sin(\angle B/2 + \theta) \cos (\angle B/2 + \theta)
&= \frac{1}{2} \sin (\angle B + 2\theta)
\end{align*}
which is maximized at  $\frac{1}{2}$ .
------
Thus to finish we have,
\begin{align*}
[AI_1I_2] \geq AB \cdot AC \cdot \sin A/2 \sin B/2 \sin C/2
\end{align*}
Recall that  $\sin A/2 = \sqrt{\frac{(s - b)(s - c)}{bc}}$  and cyclic statements hold so that,
\begin{align*}
[AI_1I_2] \geq \frac{(s - a)(s - b)(s - c)}{a} = \boxed{126}.
\end{align*}

