Note that the angles forms by the diagonals of a cyclic quadrilateral are just half the sum of the angles of the corresponding sides in the circle. Given  \(\sin\varphi_A=\frac{2}{3}\), \(\sin\varphi_B=\frac{3}{5}\), and \(\sin\varphi_C=\frac{6}{7}\), we can derive the following (order doesn't matter):

\begin{align*}
\sin(\theta_a+\theta_b) = \sin(\theta_c+\theta_d) = \frac{2}{3} 
\sin(\theta_a+\theta_c) = \sin(\theta_b+\theta_d) = \frac{3}{5} 
\sin(\theta_a+\theta_d) = \sin(\theta_b+\theta_c) = \frac{6}{7} 
\end{align*}

Notice the area of the quadrilaterals is equal to   $$ \frac{1}{2}*2\sin(\theta_a)*2\sin(\theta_b)\sin(\theta_c+\theta_d)+\frac{1}{2}*2\sin(\theta_c)*2\sin(\theta_d)\sin(\theta_a+\theta_b) = \frac{4}{3}[\sin(\theta_a)\sin(\theta_b)+\sin(\theta_c)\sin(\theta_d)] $$   through the formula  $[\triangle{ABC}]=\frac{1}{2}AB\sin{C}$  and the Law of Sines.

Now use the Product-To-Sum formula to obtain   $$ \frac{4}{3}[\cos(\theta_a-\theta_b)-\cos(\theta_a+\theta_b)+\cos(\theta_c-\theta_d)-\cos(\theta_c+\theta_d)] = \frac{4}{3}[\cos(\theta_a-\theta_b)+\cos(\theta_c-\theta_d)] $$   because  $\theta_a+\theta_b=180^{\circ}-\theta_c+\theta_d$ Now use the Sum-To-Product formula to obtain   $$ \frac{4}{3}[\cos(\frac{\theta_a-\theta_b+\theta_c-\theta_d}{2})*\cos(\frac{\theta_a-\theta_b-\theta_c+\theta_d)}{2}]=\frac{4}{3}[\sin(\theta_a+\theta_c)*\sin(\theta_a+\theta_d)] $$   because  $\theta_a+\theta_b+\theta_c+\theta_d = 180^{\circ}$ .

Finally complete the product to obtain   $$ \frac{4}{3}[\sin(\theta_a+\theta_c)*\sin(\theta_a+\theta_d)]=\frac{4}{3}*\frac{3}{5}*\frac{6}{7}=\frac{24}{35} \Rightarrow{} \boxed{059} $$

