<details><summary>motivation</summary>Initially thought to let  $p_n$  be the probability that there was no  $1, 2, 3$  sequence in the first  $n$  flips (because each sequence must end with a sequence with no  $1, 2, 3$  followed by  $1, 2, 3$ ). However,  $p_1+p_2+\cdots{} \neq 1$ , so there really isn't anything "useful" by defining  $p_n$  like this. However, if we let  $p_n$  be the probability that there was no  $1, 2, 3$  sequence in the first  $n-3$  flips, and a  $1, 2, 3$  at the end,  $p_1+p_2+\cdots{} = 1$ . Now the question becomes, how do we evaluate  $p_n$  based on other values of  $p$ ?</details>

<details><summary>solution</summary>Let  $p_n$  be the probability that there was no  $1, 2, 3$  sequence in the first  $n-3$  flips, and a  $1, 2, 3$  at the end. Then, to calculate  $p_n$ , since the sequence must end in a  $1, 2, 3$ . So a factor of  $\frac{1}{216}$  must be added. Now we want a sequence length  $n-3$  such that no  $1, 2, 3$  appear anywhere. What is the probability? Well it is  $1-\Sigma_{k=1}^{n-3}{p_k}$  (recall the property of  $p_k$ ). Therefore,  $$ p_n = \frac{1-\Sigma_{k=1}^{n-3}{p_k}}{216} $$  Well, the numerator is kinda ugly, so maybe  $p_{n-1}$  will have the same layout.  $$ p_{n-1} = \frac{1-\Sigma_{k=1}^{n-4}{p_k}}{216} $$  Combining, we have  $$ p_n = p_{n-1} - \frac{p_{n-3}}{216} $$  Note that for  $n \ge 4$ ,  $p_n = p_{n-1} - \frac{p_{n-3}}{216}$  holds and  $p_0 = p_1 = p_2 = 0$ . We want to find  $p_1+p_3+p_5+\cdots{}$ .

Naturally, we take this sum:  $$ p_5+p_7+p_9+p_{11}+\cdots{} = p_4+p_6+p_8+p_{10}+\cdots-\frac{p_2+p_4+p_6+p_8+\cdots{}}{216} $$  Note that  $p_1+p_2+p_3+\cdots{} = 1$ , so let  $p_3+p_5+p_7+p_9+p_{11}+\cdots{} = x$ .
Then,
\begin{align*}
x - \frac{1}{216} &= 1-x - \frac{1-x}{216} 
x &= \boxed{\frac{216}{431}}
\end{align*}</details>

