We begin by showing that either  $AP = CP$  or  $BP = DP$ . Indeed, if  $t$  is the sine of (either) angle formed by  $\overline{AC}$  and  $\overline{BD}$ , then  $[APB] + [CPD] = [BPC] + [APD]$  reduces to
\[AP \cdot BP \cdot t + CP \cdot DP \cdot t = BP \cdot CP \cdot t + AP \cdot DP \cdot t \implies t(AP - CP)(BP - DP) = 0\]
so either  $AP = CP$  or  $BP = DP$ , as  $t \neq 0$ . Without loss of generality suppose  $AP = CP$ .

Since  $AP = CP$ , the triangles  $ABD$  and  $CDB$  have equal areas. Moreover,  $AB = DC$  and  $BD = DB$ , but the triangles are not congruent (as  $AD \neq CB$ ). Thus  $\angle ABD + \angle CDB = 180^{\circ}$ .

Let  $C'$  be the point such that  $C'$  lies on the same side of  $\overline{BD}$  as  $C$  and  $\triangle CBD \cong \triangle C'DB$ . Then
\[\angle ABD + \angle DBC' = \angle ABD + \angle BDC = 180^{\circ},\]
so the points  $A$ ,  $B$ ,  $C'$  are collinear.

Observe  $AD = 2 \sqrt{65}$ ,  $C'D = CB = 14$ ,  $AC' = AB + BC' = 10 + 10 = 20$ , and note that
\[[ABCD] = [ABD] + [CDB] = [ABD] + [C'BD] = [AC'D].\]
Thus the desired area is the area of a triangle with sides  $2 \sqrt{65}$ ,  $14$ ,  $20$ . Such a triangle can be split into right  $2 \hbox{ -- } 16 \hbox{ -- } 2 \sqrt{65}$  and  $12 \hbox{ -- } 16 \hbox{ -- } 20$  triangles, so the altitude to the side of length  $14$  is  $16$ , and the desired area is  $\tfrac{1}{2} \cdot 14 \cdot 16 = \boxed{112}$ .

