<details><summary>Solution</summary>Let  $O$ ,  $O_1$ , and  $O_2$  be the centers of  $\Omega$ ,  $\omega_1$ , and  $\omega_2$  respectively.  It is well-known that  $T_1$ ,  $O_1$ , and  $O$  lie on a single line, as do  $T_2$ ,  $O_2$ , and  $O$ . This means that  $O_1O=13-5=8$ , and similarly  $O_2O=8$ .  Combining this with  $OT_1=OT_2$  yields  $\triangle OO_1O_2\sim\triangle OT_1T_2$ .  Since  $O_1O_2=10$ , we have \[\dfrac{OO_1}{OT_1}=\dfrac{O_1O_2}{T_1T_2}\quad\implies\quad T_1T_2=O_1O_2\cdot\dfrac{OT_1}{OO_1}=10\cdot\dfrac {13}8=\dfrac{65}{4}.\] Hence the requested answer is  $65+4=\boxed{69}$ .</details>

