<details><summary>solution</summary>We will use **<span style="color:#741B47">complex bash</span>** to solve the problem.
Let  $O$  be the origin of the complex plane and  $\Gamma$  be the unit circle.

It is well-known that  $K$  is the center of the nine-point circle. So we first get  $A,B,C$  are at  $a,b,c$  with  $\overline{a}=\frac{1}{a}, \overline{b}=\frac{1}{b}, \overline{c}=\frac{1}{c}$ ,  $K$  is at  $k=\frac{1}{2}(a+b+c)$  and  $H$  is at  $h=a+b+c$ . Also let  $F$  be the midpoint of  $\overline{AC}$  at  $f=\frac{1}{2}(a+c)$ .

We will first compute points  $L$  at  $l$  and  $M$  at  $m$ .
Since  $BL \perp BO$  we have  $\frac{b-l}{b} + \frac{\overline{b-l}}{\overline{b}}=0$ . Use  $\overline{b}=\frac{1}{b}$  and rearrange to get   $$ 2b-l-b^2\overline{l}=0 \hspace{35pt} (\clubsuit) $$  Moreover, since  $LF \perp AC$  we have 
\begin{align*}
\frac{l-f}{a-c}+\frac{\overline{l-f}}{\overline{a-c}}&=0 
\frac{2l-(a+c)}{2(a-c)}+\frac{\overline{l}-\frac{1}{2}(a+c)}{\frac{1}{a}-\frac{1}{c}}&=0 
\frac{2l-(a+c)}{2(a-c)}-\left(\frac{2ac\overline{l}-(c+a)}{2(a-c)}\right)&=0 
\Leftrightarrow 2l-ac\overline{l}=0 \hspace{35pt} (\spadesuit) 
\end{align*}
Combining  $(\clubsuit)$  and  $(\spadesuit)$  we get that 
\begin{align*}
\overline{l}&=\frac{2b}{ac+b^2} 
l &= \frac{2abc}{ac+b^2}
\end{align*}
Due to symmetry we also obtain 
\begin{align*}
    \overline{m}&=\frac{2c}{ab+c^2} 
    m&=\frac{2abc}{ab+c^2}
\end{align*}
Now we have to prove that  $LM \perp AK$ , i.e.  $\frac{a-k}{m-l}+\frac{\overline{a-k}}{\overline{m-l}}=0$ .
We compute the first summand  $S_1=\frac{a-k}{m-l}$ .
\begin{align*}
    S_1&=\frac{a-\frac{1}{2}(a+b+c)}{\frac{2abc}{ab+c^2}-\frac{2abc}{ac+b^2}} 
    &= \frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{(a-b-c)(ab+c^2)(ac+b^2)}{(2abc)(a(c-b)-(c-b)(c+b))}\right) 
    &=\frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{(ab+c^2)(ac+b^2)}{(2abc)(c-b)}\right)
\end{align*}
Now  $S_2=\frac{\overline{a-k}}{\overline{m-l}}$ .
\begin{align*}
    S_2&=\frac{\frac{1}{a}-\frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{1}{a}+\frac{1}{b}+\frac{1}{c}\right)}{\frac{2c}{ab+c^2}-\frac{2b}{ac+b^2}} 
    &=\frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{\frac{1}{a}-\frac{1}{b}-\frac{1}{c}}{2c(ac+b^2)-2b(ab+c^2)}\right) 
    &=\frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{(bc-ac-ab)(ab+c^2)(ac+b^2)}{(2abc)(a(c-b)(c+b)-bc(c-b))}\right) 
    &=-\frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{(ab+c^2)(ac+b^2)}{(2abc)(c-b)}\right)
\end{align*}
Therefore  $S_1+S_2=0$  and thus  $LM\perp AK$ , which concludes the problem.  $\square$</details>

