i AB - n^2q &= {\color{red}-n q^{2/3} \delta - n q^{1/3} \varepsilon} + \delta \varepsilon.  \end{align*} (For clarity, we highlighted in red terms which are bigger than  $O(1)$ .) Now we define two integers  $X$  and  $Y$  such that  egin{align*} 	X &= (A^3-n^3q) - 3n(AB-n^2q)  	&= 3nq^{1/3} \delta^2 - \delta^3 + {\color{red} 3n^2 q^{1/3} \varepsilon} - 3n \delta \varepsilon.  	Y &= qX + (B^3-n^3q)  	&= 3nq^{4/3} \delta^2 - q \delta^3 - 3nq\delta\varepsilon 	+ 3nq^{2/3} \varepsilon^2 - \varepsilon^3  	&= 3n \left( q^{4/3} \delta^2  - q\delta\varepsilon + q^{2/3}\varepsilon^2  ight) 	- \varepsilon^3 - q \delta^3. \end{align*} Because  $\delta$  and  $\varepsilon$  are  $O(n^{-1/2})$ , it is evident that  $Y < 1$  for sufficiently large  $n$ . However, we contend  $Y > 0$  for sufficiently large  $n$  as well:  egin{align*} 	Y &= 		3n \left( q^{4/3} \delta^2  - q\delta\varepsilon + q^{2/3}\varepsilon^2  ight) 		- \varepsilon^3 - q \delta^3.  	&\overset{	ext{AMGM}}{\ge} 		 rac32 n (q^{4/3} \delta^2 + q^{2/3} \varepsilon^2) 		- \varepsilon^3 - q \delta^3  	&= \delta^2\left(  rac 32 n q^{4/3} - q \delta ight) 	+ \varepsilon^2 \left(  rac 32 n q^{2/3} - \varepsilon ight) \end{align*} which is positive for large  $n$ .

