This proves our claim.  $\square$ Now by CRT, we can find a constant  $m$  such that all of  $p_m,p_{m+n},p_{m + 2n},\ldots$  are co prime to both  $k,n$ , where   $$ n = \prod_{p \mid k} p^2 $$   Define  $q_i = p_{m + n(i-1)} ~ \forall ~ i \ge 1$  and  $k ' = k \cdot n$ . Observe that:
[list]
- $q_i^{1008} \le l + k' \cdot i$  for all  $i$ , where  $l$  is some fixed constant.
- If  $q_i = q_j = q$ , then  $q^{1008} \mid n(i - j) \implies q^{1008} \mid i-j$  (as  $\gcd(q,n) = 1$ ), in particular  $|i - j| \ge q^{1008}$ .

Now for a sufficiently large  $s$ , look at the set   $$ X = \{q_1,q_2,\ldots,q_{s^{1007}} \} $$  By first condition, all elements of  $X$  are  $\le s$ . For each  $j \in \{2,3,\ldots,s\}$ , denote by  $b_j$  the number of time  $j$  occurs in  $X$ . By second condition we must have   $$ (b_j - 1) \cdot j^{1008} \le s^{1007} \qquad \forall~  2 \le j \le s $$  But we also have  $b_2 + b_3 + \cdots + b_s = s^{1007}$ . This gives 
\begin{align*}
s^{1007} - s &\le \sum_{j=2}^s (b_j - 1) \le \sum_{j=2}^s \frac{s^{1007}}{j^{1008}}
\end{align*}
So to obtain our contradiction, it suffices to show  $\displaystyle \sum_{j \ge 2} \frac{1}{j^{1008}} < 1 - \epsilon$  for some fixed  $\epsilon > 0$ . This just follows by  $\frac{1}{1^2} + \frac{1}{2^2} + \cdots = \frac{\pi^2}{6}$ . But for an elementary proof, note
\begin{align*}
\sum_{j \ge 2} \frac{1}{j^{1008}} &\le \sum_{j \ge 2} \frac{1}{j^3} = \sum_{i \ge 1} \left( \sum_{j=2^i}^{2^{i+1}} \frac{1}{j^3} \right) \le   \sum_{i \ge 1} \left( \frac{2^i}{2^{3i}} \right) = \sum_{i \ge 1} \frac{1}{4^i} = \frac{1}{3}
\end{align*}
So we obtain our desired contradiction, completing the proof.  $\blacksquare$

We will split our proof into two parts.**<span style="color:#0C343D">Proving when  $k$  is a prime power.</span>** Let  $k=p^{e+1}$  and fix large  $t$ . 

We will analyse how many  $p^t>n>m \geq 0$  are there such that  $\nu_p \binom{n}m \leq r$ . We will prove that this has density  $0$ . 

By Kummer's theorem, there are atmost  $e$  digits of  $m$  which is more than the corresponding digits of  $n$  in base  $p$ . 

So let  $\ell \geq 0$  be number of starting digits of  $n$  which match with starting digits of  $m$  and so finally see that the number of such  $(n,m)$  is \begin{align*}
& \sum_{\ell=0}^{t-1} \binom p2 \sum_{i=0}^e \binom{t-\ell-1}i \binom p2 ^i \left[ \binom p2 +p \right]^{t-\ell-1-i} 
\leq & 1.01^t \binom p2 ^t \sum_{\ell=0}^{t-1} \sum_{i=0}^e \frac{\binom{t-\ell-1}i}{\binom p2 ^\ell} 
\leq & \left[1.01 \binom p2 \right]^t \sum_{\ell=0}^{t-1} \binom p2 ^{-\ell} \left((t-\ell-1)^0+\dots+(t-\ell-1)^e \right) 
\leq & 2\left[1.01 \binom p2 \right]^t \sum_{\ell=0}^{t-1} (t-\ell-1)^e \binom p2 ^{-\ell} 
\leq & 2\left[1.01 \binom p2 \right]^t \sum_{\ell=0}^{t-1} 1.01^t \binom p2 ^{-\ell} 
\leq & 2t\left[1.01^2 \binom p2 \right]^t \leq t(0.6p^2)^t
\end{align*} Now see that \[\frac{t(0.6p^2)^t}{p^{2t}} =t \left(\frac 35 \right)^t \sim 0\] And so see that for any  $\varepsilon>0$  and any  $e \geq 0$ , there exists large enough  $t$  such that for  $1-\varepsilon$  density of pairs  $(n,m)$  such that  $p^t > n \geq m \geq 0$  we have  $p^{e+1} \mid \binom nm$  (see that if  $m=n$ , then there are  $p^t$  such pairs which is  $o(p^{2t})$  anyways). 

And hence if we replaces  $0.99$  with  $1-\delta$  in the original question, then also due to our previous statement, the conclusion holds true. **<span style="color:#0C343D">Extending to all  $k$ .</span>** Just evaluate the prime factorization of  $k$  and do the process in the first part to each of the prime power divisors of  $k$  and since  $\delta$  can be as small as possible, the probability of success of all these prime powers combines together will eventually be atleast  $99 \%$  and we win. **<span style="color:#274E13">
Remark:</span>** One might say in first part, we are only taking the upperbound as some prime power, but since this is true for eventually all prime powers; we get that if we take the upperbound to be some large enough  $N$  which isn't a prime power then also we are going to be fine anyways.

Let  $I$  be the incenter of  $\triangle ABC$ , and let  $X$  and  $Y$  be the Miquel points of quadrilaterals  $BEDC$  and  $CFDB$ , respectively.

We first locate  $X$  and  $Y$ . By definition  $B,D,E,X$  are cyclic, and a spiral similarity at  $X$  sends  $\overline{DC}$  to  $\overline{BE}$ . But  $DC = BE$ , so this is just a rotation. Therefore, by symmetry  $B,I,X$  collinear. Similarly,  $C,I,Y$  collinear and  $C,D,F,Y$  cyclic. Moreover,  $X$  and  $Y$  are fixed as they both lie on the perpendicular bisector of  $\overline{BC}$ .

Finally, note that  $$ \measuredangle PXI = \measuredangle PXB = \measuredangle PDB = \measuredangle PDC = \measuredangle PYC = \measuredangle PYI, $$  so  $I,P,X,Y$  cyclic, as desired. ( $Q$  is the center of  $(IXY)$ .)

