We claim that there are no solutions to this equation.
Let  $d= \gcd(xy+1,xy+x+2)= \gcd(x+1,y-1)$ . Let  $xy+1=da^2,xy+x+2=db^2$  for positive integers  $a,b$ . Observe that  $0<b^2-a^2<a^2 \implies a<b<a \sqrt2$ . Also note that  $x+1=d(b^2-a^2)$ , so we have  $y=\frac{da^2-1}{d(b^2-a^2)-1} \implies dyb^2-(dy+d)a^2=y-1 \implies (by)^2-y(y+1)a^2=\frac{y(y-1)}{d}$ Consider the following two Pell equations, calling them the “ $1$ -pell” and the “ $A$ -pell” respectively
\begin{align*}
X^2-y(y+1)Y^2=1
X^2-y(y+1)Y^2=\frac{y(y-1)}{d}
\end{align*}
Observe that  $(2y+1,2)$  is the smallest solution to the “ $1$ -pell”. $Claim.$   $(by,a)$  is the smallest solution to the “ $A$ -pell” $Proof.$  Indeed, suppose  $(u,v)$  is the smallest solution instead. Then note that  $v \ge 1$  and   $$ u \ge \sqrt{y(y+1)+\frac{y(y-1)}{d}} $$  We also know that  $(by,a)$  is a solution, so we must have  $$ by+a \sqrt{y(y+1)}=(u+v \sqrt{y(y+1)})(2y+1+2 \sqrt{y(y+1)})^i $$  for some  $i \in \mathbb{Z}$ . Then we can bound to get
\begin{align*}
by+a \sqrt{y(y+1)}&=(u+v \sqrt{y(y+1)})(2y+1+2 \sqrt{y(y+1)})^i
& \ge (\sqrt{y(y+1)+\frac{y(y-1)}{d}}+\sqrt{y(y+1)})(4y)
&\ge 2y(4y)=8y^2
\end{align*}
Observe that   $$ by+a \sqrt{y(y+1)}<y(a+b) +\frac{a}{2}  $$  See that
\begin{align*}
&8y^2>y(a+b)+\frac{a}{2}
 \iff & 8y^2-y(a+b)-\frac{a}{2}>0
 \iff & y> \frac{a+b+\sqrt{(a+b)^2+16a} }{16a} 
\end{align*}
See that 
\begin{align*}
\frac{a+b+\sqrt{(a+b)^2+16a} }{16a} < \frac{a+b+(a+b+4)}{16a}=\frac{a+b+2}{8a}
\end{align*}
We already know that  $y=\frac{da^2-1}{d(b^2-a^2)-1}>\frac{a^2}{a^2-b^2}$ . We want to show  $$ \frac{a^2}{b^2-a^2}>\frac{a+b+2}{8a} \iff 8a^3>(b^2-a^2)(a+b+2) $$  which is correct, since  $$ (b^2-a^2)(a+b+2)<a^2(3a+2)<8a^3 $$  Therefore, we have  $$ 8y^2<by+a<y(a+b)+2<8y^2 $$  which is contradictory.

Now that we know that  $(by,a)$  is the fundamental solution, making use of goated Pell inequalities, we must have  $$ by+a \sqrt{y(y+1)}<\sqrt{\frac{y(y-1)}{d}}(2y+1+2\sqrt{y(y+1)} $$  This implies 
\begin{align*}
b\sqrt{\frac{y}{y+1}}+a&=\frac{by+a \sqrt{y(y+1)}}{\sqrt{y(y+1)}}
&<\frac{\sqrt{\frac{y(y-1)}{d}}(2y+1+2\sqrt{y(y+1)}}{\sqrt{y(y+1)} }
&=\sqrt{\frac{y(y-1)}{d} }\left( \frac{2y+1}{\sqrt{y(y+1)}} +2\right) 
&<4 \sqrt{\frac{y(y-1)}{d} }
\end{align*}
Observe that  $$ y=\frac{da^2-1}{d(b^2-a^2)-1}<\frac{a^2-1}{b^2-a^2-1}<\frac{a^2-1}{2a} \approx \frac{a}{2} $$  The  $\approx$  symbol works since  $y$  is a positive integer. So we have  $$ b\sqrt{\frac{y}{y+1}}+a>2y\left(\sqrt{\frac{y}{y+1}}+1 \right) $$  This implies that  $$ 2y \left(\sqrt{\frac{y}{y+1}}+1 \right) < 4 \sqrt{\frac{y(y-1)}{d} }<4\sqrt{y(y-1)} $$   This implies that  $$ y^2 \left( 1+\frac{y}{y+1}+2\sqrt{\frac{y}{y+1}} \right)=2y-1+\frac{1+2y\sqrt{y(y+1)}}{y+1}<4y $$  This gives us  $$ \frac{2y^2+1}{y+1}< \frac{1+2y\sqrt{y(y+1)}}{y+1}<2y-3 $$  which is absurd (simply cross multiply), so no  $x,y$  exists.

