<details><summary>Solution</summary>I claim that  $C=2+\sqrt{2}$  works.

First we introduce two easy-to-prove lemmas:
<details><summary>Two lemmas</summary>(1)  $H(n)\leqslant \ln n+1.$ (2)  $n\ln n-n+1\leqslant \ln(n!).$</details>
Now, 
\begin{align*}
& \sum_{i=1}^mH(a_i)\leqslant \left(2+\sqrt{2}\right)\left(\sum_{i=1}^mia_i\right)^{\frac{1}{2}}  
\iff & m+\ln (a_1\cdots a_m)\leqslant \left(2+\sqrt{2}\right)\left(\sum_{i=1}^mia_i\right)^{\frac{1}{2}} \text{ by Lemma 1}  
\iff & \ln (a_1\cdots a_m)\leqslant 2\left(\sum_{i=1}^mia_i\right)^{\frac{1}{2}} \quad\text{ because } m<\sqrt{2}\sqrt{\frac{m(m+1)}{2}}\leqslant \sqrt{2}\left(\sum_{i=1}^mia_i\right)^{\frac{1}{2}}
\end{align*}
Let  $t = \sum_{i=1}^mia_i>\frac{m^2}{2}.$  By AM-GM,  $$ t \geqslant m\left(\prod_{i=1}^mia_i\right)^{\frac{1}{m}} = m\left(m! \prod_{i=1}^ma_i\right)^{\frac{1}{m}}. $$  Thus,  $$ \prod_{i=1}^ma_i\leqslant \frac{t^m}{m!m^m}. $$  By Lemma 2, we obtain  $$ \ln (a_1\cdots a_m)\leqslant m\ln t-m\ln m-\ln m!<m\ln t-2m\ln m+m. $$  It's suffice to show  $4t\geqslant \left(m\ln t-2m\ln m+m\right)^2,\quad t>\frac{m^2}{2}.$ The rest is not hard. Good luck!</details>

