for(int i=0; i<names.length; ++i){
dot(names[i], pts[i], dir(labels[i]));
}
[/asy]
By similar triangles,  $PN=2CM$ . Hence  $N$  lies on  $(ABC)$  translated by the vector  $2\vec{MC}$ .
(Note that we may eliminate essentially all of the assumptions of the problem. All that is important is that  $CM\parallel PN$  and  $\frac{CQ}{QP}$  is fixed.)

[asy]
import olympiad;
pair O=(0,0), A=(-sqrt(3)/2, 1/2), C=(0,1), B=(sqrt(3)/2, 1/2), M=(0, 1/2), P=(3/5, 4/5), Q=(1/5, 14/15), N=(3/5, 9/5), R=(3/5,1/2);
draw(circumcircle(A,B,C));
draw(A--B--C--cycle);
label(" $B$ ", B, dir(30));
label(" $C$ ", C, N);
label(" $A$ ", A, dir(150));
label(" $M$ ", M, SW);
label(" $O$ ", O, S);
dot(O);
label(" $P$ ", P, NE);
label(" $Q$ ", Q, dir(15));
label(" $N$ ", N, N);
draw(M--N--R);
draw(P--C);
dot(Q);
dot(M);
draw(rightanglemark(N, R, A, 2));
[/asy]

We proceed with complex bash. Let the circumcenter of  $ABC$  be  $O,$  and let the complex number representing each point on the complex plane be its lowercase counterpart (e.g.  $A$  is paired with  $a,$  etc.) Futhermore, let  $o=0$  and let the circumcircle of  $ABC$  be the unit circle with  $c=i.$  Then,  $b=\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}+\dfrac{i}{2}$  and  $a=-\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}+\dfrac{i}{2},$  so  $m=\dfrac{a+b}{2}=\dfrac{i}{2}.$ Note that  $q$  is the complex number with lies  $\dfrac{1}{3}$  of the way from  $c$  to  $p,$  so  $q=\dfrac{2c+p}{3}=\dfrac{p+2i}{3},$  so since  $N$  lies on line  $QM,$   $\dfrac{n-q}{n-m}=\dfrac{n-\dfrac{p}{3}-\dfrac{2i}{3}}{n-\dfrac{i}{2}}$  is real.

As such,  $\dfrac{n-\dfrac{p}{3}-\dfrac{2i}{3}}{n-\dfrac{i}{2}}=\overline{\left(\dfrac{n-\dfrac{p}{3}-\dfrac{2i}{3}}{n-\dfrac{i}{2}}\right)}=\dfrac{\overline{n}-\dfrac{\overline{p}}{3}+\dfrac{2i}{3}}{\overline{n}+\dfrac{i}{2}},$  and since  $p$  lies on the unit circle,  $\overline{p}=\dfrac{1}{p},$  so  $\dfrac{n-\dfrac{p}{3}-\dfrac{2i}{3}}{n-\dfrac{i}{2}}=\dfrac{\overline{n}-\dfrac{\overline{p}}{3}+\dfrac{2i}{3}}{\overline{n}+\dfrac{i}{2}}=\dfrac{\overline{n}-\dfrac{1}{3p}+\dfrac{2i}{3}}{\overline{n}+\dfrac{i}{2}},$  so  $n\overline{n}-\dfrac{p\overline{n}}{3}-\dfrac{2i\overline{n}}{3}+\dfrac{ni}{2}-\dfrac{pi}{6}+\dfrac{1}{3}=n\overline{n}-\dfrac{n}{3p}+\dfrac{2in}{3}-\dfrac{\overline{n}i}{2}+\dfrac{i}{6p}+\dfrac{1}{3},$  so  $n\left(\dfrac{1}{3p}-\dfrac{i}{6}\right)-\overline{n}\left(\dfrac{p}{3}+\dfrac{i}{6}\right)=\dfrac{pi}{6}+\dfrac{i}{6p}.$ Since  $NP$  is perpendicular to  $BA,$  we have that  $\dfrac{n-p}{b-a}=\dfrac{n-p}{\sqrt{3}}$  is a pure imaginary number, so  $n-p$  is pure imaginary, so  $n-p+\overline{n-p}=n-p+\overline{n}-\overline{p}=0,$  so  $n+\overline{n}=p+\overline{p}=p+\dfrac{1}{p}. $  Then,  $n\left(\dfrac{p}{3}+\dfrac{i}{6}\right)+\overline{n}\left(\dfrac{p}{3}+\dfrac{i}{6}\right)=\left(\dfrac{p}{3}+\dfrac{i}{6}\right)\left(p+\dfrac{1}{p}\right)=\dfrac{p^2}{3}+\dfrac{1}{3}+\dfrac{pi}{6}+\dfrac{i}{6p},$  so  $n\left(\dfrac{p}{3}+\dfrac{1}{3p}\right)=\left(n\left(\dfrac{p}{3}+\dfrac{i}{6}\right)+\overline{n}\left(\dfrac{p}{3}+\dfrac{i}{6}\right)\right)+\left(n\left(\dfrac{1}{3p}-\dfrac{i}{6}\right)-\overline{n}\left(\dfrac{p}{3}+\dfrac{i}{6}\right)\right)=\left(\dfrac{p^2}{3}+\dfrac{1}{3}+\dfrac{pi}{6}+\dfrac{i}{6p}\right)+\left(\dfrac{pi}{6}+\dfrac{i}{6p}\right)=\dfrac{p^2}{3}+\dfrac{1}{3}+\dfrac{pi}{3}+\dfrac{i}{3p},$  so  $n\left(p+\dfrac{1}{p}\right)=p^2+1+pi+\dfrac{i}{p}=\left(p+\dfrac{1}{p}\right)(p+i),$  so  $n=p+i,$  and  $|n-i|=|p|=1,$  so  $N$  lies on the circle centered at  $C$  with radius  $1,$  as desired.  $\Box$

