More algebra than number theory really. The answer is  $a_{2m} \leq 2^m - 1$ .**<span style="color:blue">Construction:**</span> Take  $a_{2m} = 2^m - 1$  and  $a_{2m-1} = 1$  for each positive integer  $m$ . This can be easily checked.**<span style="color:blue">Bound:**</span> We do some algebra magic. Let  $S_n = a_1+a_2+\cdots+a_{2n}$  for each  $n \geq 1$ . The key claim is the following:**<span style="color:#f00">Claim:**</span>  $S_{n+1} \leq 2S_n + 2$  for every  $n$ .

*Proof:* Observe that  $a_{2n+1} \mid a_{2n+2} - a_1$ , i.e.  $a_{2n+1}$  and  $a_{2n+2}$  must be relatively prime. In particular,  $a_{2n+1}a_{2n+2} \mid a_1+a_2+\cdots+a_{2n+1}$ . In particular,
\begin{align*}
S_{n+1} &\leq a_1+a_2+\cdots+a_{2n+1} + \frac{a_1+a_2+\cdots+a_{2n+1}}{a_{2n+1}} 
&= 1 + S_n + a_{2n+1} + \frac{S_n}{a_{2n+1}} 
&\leq 2S_n+2. \ \blacksquare
\end{align*}

Since  $S_1 = 2$  as  $a_2 = 1$  is forced, it follows that  $S_n \leq 2^{n+1} - 2$  for each  $n$  by induction. Now, since  $a_{2n} \mid a_1+a_2+\cdots+a_{2n-1}$ , it follows  $a_{2n} \leq \tfrac 12 S_n \leq 2^n - 1$ , as needed.

