I claim that the pair  $(b, c) = \left(m^2, m(a_1+a_2+\cdots+a_m) - 1\right)$  works unless all the  $a_i$  are equal, in which case the problem is trivial. In particular, the key claim is the following. **<span style="color:#f00">Claim:**</span> For sufficiently large integers  $n$ , the chain of inequalities \[\sqrt{bn+c} < \sqrt{n+a_1}+\sqrt{n+a_2}+\cdots+\sqrt{n+a_m} < \sqrt{bn+c+1}\] holds.

*Proof:* The right hand inequality is evident by QM-AM. Now, we will upper bound the quantity 
\begin{align*}
D &= m\sqrt{n+\frac{a_1+a_2+\cdots+a_m}m} - \sqrt{n+a_1}-\sqrt{n+a_2}-\cdots-\sqrt{n+a_m} 
&= \frac{m^2n+ma_1+ma_2+\cdots+ma_m - \left(\sqrt{n+a_1}+\sqrt{n+a_2}+\cdots+\sqrt{n+a_m}\right)^2}{\sqrt{n+a_1}+\cdots+\sqrt{n+a_m} + \sqrt{m^2n+m(a_1+a_2+\cdots+a_m)}} 
&\leq \frac{\sum_{i<j} \left(\sqrt{n+a_i} - \sqrt{n+a_j}\right)^2}{2m\sqrt n} 
&\leq \frac{\sum_{i<j} \left(\frac{a_i-a_j}{2\sqrt n}\right)^2}{2m\sqrt n} 
&= \frac{\sum_{i<j} (a_i-a_j)^2}{8mn^{3/2}}.
\end{align*}
Let  $A \neq 0$  denote the numerator of this expression. Check that on the other hand, 
\[\sqrt{bn+c+1} - \sqrt{bn+c} = \frac 1{\sqrt{bn+c+1}+\sqrt{bn+c}} \geq \frac 1{2\sqrt{(b+1)n}}\] for  $n$  sufficiently large. So for all  $n > \frac{A\left(m^2+1\right)^{1/2}}{4m}$ , we get the chain of inequalities, as needed.  $\blacksquare$ Now notice that for any real number  $r \in \left(\sqrt{bn+c}, \sqrt{bn+c+1}\right)$ , we always have  $\lfloor r \rfloor = \left \lfloor \sqrt{bn+c} \right \rfloor$  as no integer may fall within this interval. Thus we get the result.**<span style="color:purple">Remark:**</span> This solution essentially showcases the power of [Lagrange's identity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lagrange%27s_identity) as a sort of ``QM-AM error bound".

Let  $S=\sum a_i$ . If  $a_1=a_2=\dots=a_m$ , then clearly  $b=m^2, c=Sm$  works. We claim that otherwise,  $b=m^2, c=Sm-1$  works. It suffices to show   $$ \sqrt{m^2n+Sm-1} < \sum _{i=1} ^m \sqrt{n+a_i} < \sqrt{m^2n+Sm}. $$   The upper bound follows from Jensen's on  $f(x)=\sqrt{n+x}$ , which is concave. For the lower bound, note that by AM-GM, it suffices to show 
\begin{align*}
m\sqrt[m]{\prod (n+a_i)} \ge m \sqrt[2m]{\prod(n+a_i)} \ge \sqrt{m^2n+Sm-1} 
\end{align*}
for sufficiently large  $n$ . This can be rearranged as 
\begin{align*}
m^{2m}\prod (n+a_i) - (m^2n+Sm-1)^m \ge 0.
\end{align*}
The leading coefficient of this polynomial simplifies as  $m^{2m-1}$ , which is positive. Therefore,  $m^{2m}\prod (n+a_i) - (m^2n+Sm-1)^m$  will be nonnegative for sufficiently large  $n$ , as desired.

