Note that  $FB = FD$  and  $GC = GD$ . Let  $B'$  and  $C'$  denote the second intersections of  $\odot(F, B)$  and  $\odot(G, C)$  with  $(ABC)$ .**[color = orange] Lemma 1: [/color]**  $B'DEC'$  is cyclic with center  $A$ .

*Proof:* We have
\begin{align*}
	\angle ADB' &= 180^\circ - \angle BDB' 
	&= \frac{\angle BFB'}{2} 
	&= \frac{180^\circ - \angle BAB'}{2} 
	&= \frac{180^\circ - \angle DAB'}{2}.
\end{align*}
Hence,  $AB' = AD$ . Similarly,  $AC' = AE$ , so  $B'DEC'$  is cyclic with center  $A$ , as claimed.  $\blacksquare$ **[color = orange] Lemma 2: [/color]**  $B', E, G$  and  $C', D, F$  are collinear. 

*Proof:* We have
\begin{align*}
	\angle ADC' &= \frac{180^\circ - \angle DAC'}{2} 
	&= \frac{180^\circ - 2\angle DB'C'}{2} 
	&= 90^\circ - \angle DB'C' 
	&= 90^\circ - (\angle DB'A - \angle AB'C') 
	&= 90^\circ - (180^\circ - \angle BDB') + \angle AB'C' 
	&= (\angle BDB' - 90^\circ) + \angle AB'C' 
	&= \left(180^\circ - \frac{\angle BFB'}{2} - 90^\circ\right) + \angle AB'C' 
	&= \left(90^\circ - \frac{\angle BFB'}{2}\right) + \angle AB'C' 
	&= \angle FBB' + \angle AC'B' 
	&= \angle FBB' + \angle ABB' 
	&= \angle FBA 
	&= \angle FBD.
\end{align*}
Therefore,  $C', D, F$  are collinear (since  $A, D, B$  are collinear). Similarly,  $B', E, G$  are collinear.  $\blacksquare$ To conclude, we have
\begin{align*}
	\angle B'ED = \angle B'C'D = \angle B'C'F = \angle B'GF,
\end{align*}
so  $\overline{DE}$  and  $\overline{FG}$  have the same direction.  $\Box$

