Let  $M$  be the midpoint of minor arc  $BC$ .  Since  $\triangle ADE$  is isosceles,  $\overline{AM} \perp \overline{DE}$ , so it is enough to show  $\overline{AM} \perp \overline{FG}$ .

Let  $\angle FBA = \alpha$ ,  $\angle GCA = \beta$ , and  $R$  be the circumradius of  $\triangle ABC$ .  Then  $$ AB = 2R\sin(C) $$    $$ AC = 2R\sin(B) $$    $$ BD = 4R\sin(C-\alpha)\cos(\alpha) $$    $$ CE = 4R\sin(B-\beta)\cos(\beta) $$  It should be that  $AB - AC = BD - CE$ , so we can set up the equation  $$ \dfrac{\sin(C)-\sin(B)}{2} = \sin(C-\alpha)\cos(\alpha) - \sin(B-\beta)\cos(\beta) $$  By Product-to-Sum Formulas, the right side of the equation simplifies to  $$ \dfrac{\sin(C)-\sin(B)}{2} = \left(\dfrac{\sin(C) + \sin(C-2\alpha)}{2}\right) - \left(\dfrac{\sin(B) + \sin(B-2\beta)}{2}\right) $$  Simplifying, we find  $$ \sin(C - 2\alpha) = \sin(B - 2\beta) $$  It cannot be that  $C - 2\alpha + B - 2\beta = \pi$  since  $C + B < \pi$ , so we must have  $C - 2\alpha = B - 2\beta$ , or   $$ \beta - \alpha = \dfrac{B-C}{2} $$  Now we can angle chase to finish.  Let  $T$  be the intersection of  $\overline{AM}$  and  $\overline{FG}$ ; we wish to show  $\angle ATF = \dfrac{\pi}{2}$ .  We can find  $\angle FAT = C + \dfrac{A}{2} - \alpha$  and  $\angle AFT = \beta$ , so   $$ \angle FAT + \angle AFT = C + \dfrac{A}{2} + (\beta - \alpha) = C + \dfrac{A}{2} + \dfrac{B-C}{2} = \dfrac{A + B + C}{2} = \dfrac{\pi}{2} $$  as desired.

