Define  $G'$  on  $(ABC)$  such that  $\overline{FG'} \perp \ell$  where  $\ell$  is the angle bisector of  $A$ . Also, define the point  $E'$  on  $\overline{AC}$  such that  $GE' = GC$ . The goal is to show that  $E = E'$  and  $G = G'$ . **<span style="color:#9a00ff">Claim:</span>**  $\angle FBA + \frac{\angle B}{2} = \angle G'CA + \frac{\angle{C}}{2}$ .
<span style="color:#f00">**Proof:**</span> This can be shown by proving  $\angle FBA + \frac{\angle B}{2} + \frac{\angle C}{2} = \angle G'CA + \angle C$ :
\begin{align*}
\angle FBA + \frac{\angle B}{2} + \frac{\angle C}{2} &= \angle FBA+\left(90^\circ - \frac{\angle A}{2}\right)
&= \angle FBA + \angle APQ
&= 180^\circ - \angle BFG' 
&= \angle G'CB
\end{align*}
which is equal to  $\angle G'CA + \angle C$ .  $\blacksquare$ The rest can be done with trig bash. Let  $x = \angle FBA - \frac{\angle C}{2} = \angle GCA - \frac{\angle B}{2}$ .
\begin{align*}
\frac{BF}{\sin \angle FAB} = 2R \quad \Longrightarrow \quad BF &= 2R \sin \angle FAB
&= 2R\sin(\angle C - \angle FBA)
&= 2R\sin\left(\frac{\angle C}{2}-x\right).
BD=BF\cos\angle FBA \quad \Longrightarrow \quad BD &= 4R\sin\left(\frac{\angle C}{2}\right)\cos\left(\frac{\angle C}{2}+x\right)
&= 2R\left(\sin(-2x)+\sin\angle C\right) \qquad\qquad\qquad\quad \text{(product to sum)}
&= 2R\left(\sin\angle C-\sin(2x)\right).
AD = AB-BD \quad \Longrightarrow \quad AD &= (2R\sin\angle C) - 2R(\sin\angle C-\sin(2x))
&= 2R\sin(2x)
\end{align*}
Doing the same thing on the other side,  $AE'$  can also be calculated to be equal to  $2R\sin(2x)$ . Therefore,  $AD=AE'$ , meaning  $E = E'$ . It then also follows that  $G=G'$ .  $\blacksquare$ .

The answer is all  $n$  with  $3 \mid n$ . A construction is  $(-1, -1, 2, \dots -1, -1, 2).$  Now, 
\begin{align*} 
a_i a_{i + 1} a_{i + 2} + a_{i + 2} &= a_{i + 2}^2 
a_{i} a_{i + 1} a_{i + 2} + a_{i} = a_{i} a_{i + 3} 
\end{align*} whence  $\sum_{i = 1}^{n} a_{i}^2 = \sum_{i=1}^n a_{i} a_{i + 3} \implies \sum_{i = 1}^{n} (a_{i} - a_{i + 3})^2 = 0$  so  $\{a_n\}$  is periodic with period  $3$ . Now we must have   $$ \{a_n\} = (a_1, a_2, a_1a_2 + 1, a_1, a_2, a_1a_2 + 1, \dots a_1) $$   if  $n \equiv 1 \pmod{3}$  and   $$ \{a_n\} = (a_1, a_2, a_1a_2 + 1, \dots a_1, a_2) $$   if  $n \equiv 2 \pmod{3}$ . But in both cases we must have  $a_1 = a_2 = a_1a_2 + 1 = \dots = a_{n}$  which means  $a_1^2 + 1 = a_1$ , which has no real solutions, contradiction.  $\blacksquare$

Got a hint for the algebraic manipulation.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>I claim that the answer is all  $3 \mid n.$  It is easy to see that   $$ a_i=\begin{cases}
    -1 \text{ if } i \not \equiv 0 \pmod{3} 
    2 \text{ if } i \equiv 0 \pmod{3}
\end{cases} $$   indeed satisfies the given equations, so it remains to show that there are no real solutions if  $3 \nmid n.$  

Manipulating the given equations, we get
\begin{align*}
    a_ia_{i+1}+1=a_{i+2} &\implies a_ia_{i+1}a_{i+2}+a_{i+2}=a_{i+2}^2 
    &\implies a_i(a_{i+3}-1)+a_{i+2}=a_{i+2}^2 
    &\implies a_ia_{i+3}-a_i+a_{i+2}=a_{i+2}^2.
\end{align*}
If we add all of these equations, then the singular terms will cancel, and since  $3 \nmid n,$  we can re-index the terms to get a summation of the form   $$ \sum_{\text{cyc}} a_1a_2 = \sum_{\text{cyc}} a_1^2. $$   By Muirhead's Inequality, however, we know that   $$ \sum_{\text{cyc}} a_1a_2 \le \sum_{\text{cyc}} a_1^2, $$   so it follows that equality must hold, i.e.   $$ a_1=a_2=\ldots=a_n. $$   However, if we let all of them be  $x,$  then we get   $$ x^2+1=x \implies x^2-x+1=0, $$   which has no real roots, contradiction. 

Hence, we have shown that there exists a solution for all  $3 \mid n,$  and there cannot exist any real solution for  $3 \nmid n,$  as desired.  $\square$</details>

