The key observation is that no two opposite indices have the same color.

We will divide the proof further into two cases:

Case 1: Amy places a red stone at a position with the same color as a previously placed blue stone

In this case, it can be trivially observed that this new red stone would be at a distance of \( \sqrt{5} \) from a stone placed in Amy's previous turns, which leads to a contradiction.

Case 2: Amy never interferes Case1

In this case, no two red or blue stones placed in the \( 4 \times 4 \) grid will form a pair with the same color. 

This implies that Amy and Bob together can place at most 8 stones on this grid. Consequently, Amy can place at most 4 stones.

Applying the same argument to each \( 4 \times 4 \) sub-grid, we find that Amy can place at most:
\[
\frac{200}{8} \times 4 = 100
\]
stones in total.

Thus, combining both parts, we conclude that:
\[
K = 100
\]
as desired.

The answer is  $100$ .**<span style="color:#674EA7">Strategy for Ben.</span>**The key idea  partition the  $20\times 20$  set of lattice points into  $25$  pieces of  $4\times 4$  lattice points which we'll call **units** as depicted below. And then colour each of the sites in one of four colours in this manner.

	  $$ \begin{matrix}
		\textcolor{blue}{\bullet} & \textcolor{green}{\bullet} & \textcolor{red}{\bullet} & \textcolor{yellow}{\bullet}
		\textcolor{red}{\bullet} & \textcolor{yellow}{\bullet} & \textcolor{blue}{\bullet} & \textcolor{green}{\bullet}
		\textcolor{green}{\bullet} & \textcolor{blue}{\bullet} & \textcolor{yellow}{\bullet} & \textcolor{red}{\bullet}
		\textcolor{yellow}{\bullet} & \textcolor{red}{\bullet} & \textcolor{green}{\bullet} & \textcolor{blue}{\bullet}
	\end{matrix} $$  	Suppose Amy places her stone on a site, then Ben can always place his stone in the same  $4\times 4$  unit and same coloured site in which Amy had placed her stone just before in such way that Amy can place no more stones in the same coloured site in that unit. And this strategy of Ben forces Amy to not place more than  $4$  stones in each unit. Hence the upper bound of  $100$ .**<span style="color:#674EA7">Strategy for Amy.</span>** Our goal is to show that Amy can place at least  $100$  stones regardless of the wildest of strategies Ben designs. We simply colour the lattice points in checkered pattern with black and white. Then Amy can always choose to place her stones in white spots (non-attacking knights). Ben can best ruin this strategy by placing all his stones in white sites. But even then Amy would placed  $100$  of her stones. 

And we are done.  $\square$

Let's define  $b_n\coloneqq\frac{a_1}{a_2}+\frac{a_2}{a_3}+\cdots+\frac{a_{n-1}}{a_n}+\frac{a_n}{a_1}$ . Note how
    \[b_{n+1}=b_n+\frac{a_n}{a_{n+1}}+\frac{a_{n+1}-a_n}{a_1}\]
    Since  $b_n \in \mathbb{Z}, b_{n+1}\in \mathbb{Z} \implies \frac{a_n}{a_{n+1}}+\frac{a_{n+1}-a_n}{a_1} \in \mathbb{Z} \implies \nu_p\left(\frac{a_n}{a_{n+1}}+\frac{a_{n+1}-a_n}{a_1}\right)\geq 0 \quad \forall p$ . And it is strictly greater than the min if  $\nu_p\left(\frac{a_n}{a_{n+1}}\right) = \nu_p\left(\frac{a_{n+1}-a_n}{a_1}\right)$     Let now  $p$  be a generic prime number. Let's now try to prove that  $\exists K$  such that  $\nu_p(a_{n+1})=\nu_p(a_n)$ .
    Let's analyze what happens with any prime and divide them into cases. 
    \begin{align*}
        &x \coloneqq \nu_p(a_n), \qquad y \coloneqq \nu_p(a_{n+1}), \qquad q \coloneqq \nu_p(a_1)
        x \neq y& \implies \nu_p\left(\frac{a_n}{a_{n+1}}+\frac{a_{n+1}-a_n}{a_1}\right)\geq \min(x-y, \min(x.y)-q)
        x>y& \implies \min(x-y, y-q) \implies x>y\geq q \tag{1}
        x<y& \implies \min(x-y, x-q)<0 \implies x-y=x-q \implies y=q \tag{2}
        x=y \tag{3}
    \end{align*}
    Note how in (1)  $y$  is strictly decreasing and lower bounded at the same time. It implies that there exist a  $K$  such that  $\forall p, \forall n>K$  there is no  $a_n$  falling in that case ( $K>N+x-q+1$ )
    Note how in (2) (which is true because the non equality case implies that the  $\nu_p$  are equal)  $\nu_p(a_{n+1})=\nu_p(a_1)$  therefore it is constant for all  $n$  from  $N+1$  onward.
    Note how in (3) we literally get that  $\nu_p(a_n)$  is constant.

    So in all cases, therefore for all primes we've shown that there exist a  $K$  such that  $\nu_p(a_n)$  is constant. It follows immediately that also  $(a_n)$  will eventually be constant (maybe, I just read that it is from Evan and I'm confused now) :3

