We will require the following well-known lemma:**Lemma.** Point  $P$  is inside quadrilateral  $ABCD$  and has an isogonal conjugate with respect to  $ABCD$ . Then  $\angle APB + \angle CPD = 180^{\circ}$ .

Let the diagonals of  $ABCD$  meet at  $E$ . Since  $\tfrac{AB}{AD} = \tfrac{CB}{CD}$ , there exists a point  $K$  on  $\overline{BD}$  such that  $\overline{AE}$ ,  $\overline{AK}$  are isogonal in  $\angle A$  and  $\overline{CE}$ ,  $\overline{CK}$  are isogonal in  $\angle C$ . Similarly define a point  $L$  on  $\overline{AC}$ .

Define  $X$  to be the second intersection of  $(ABK)$  and  $(CDK)$ . We claim that this is the desired point  $X$ .

Note that
\[\measuredangle XAB = \measuredangle XKB = \measuredangle XKD = \measuredangle XCD\]
and defining  $Q = \overline{AD} \cap \overline{BC}$  for convenience,
\begin{align*}
\measuredangle BXD & = \measuredangle BXK + \measuredangle KXD
& = \measuredangle BAK + \measuredangle KCD
& = \measuredangle EAD + \measuredangle BCE
& = \measuredangle CAQ + \measuredangle QCA
& = \measuredangle CQA
& = \measuredangle BQD
\end{align*}
so
\[\measuredangle XBC = \measuredangle XBQ = \measuredangle XDQ = \measuredangle XDA.\]
Hence this is the correct point.

To finish, note  $\angle AXB + \angle CXD = \angle AKB + \angle CKD$ . But  $K$  and  $L$  are isogonal conjugates, so by the lemma  $\angle AKB + \angle CKD = 180^{\circ}$ .

