Since  $ABCD$  is harmonic, the bisectors of  $\angle BAD$  and  $\angle BCD$  meet at a point  $E$  on  $BD$ . Hence, there exists a point  $Y$  on  $BD$  such that  $\angle DAY = \angle BAC$  and  $\angle BCA = \angle DCY$ .

Since  $BD$  bisects  $\angle AYC$ , we have  $\angle BYA + \angle CYD = 180^\circ$ . Redefine  $X$  as the intersection, other than  $Y$ , of  $(ABY)$  and  $(CDY)$ . Then  $\angle BXA + \angle CXD = 180^\circ$ . 

Let  $BC$  and  $AD$  meet at  $F$ . By the isogonality of pairs  $(AC, AY)$  in  $\angle BAD$  and  $(CA, CY)$  in  $\angle BCD$ , we have  $\angle BXD = \angle BXY + \angle YXD = (180^\circ - \angle CAD) + \angle BCA = \angle CAF + \angle BCA = 180^\circ - \angle BFA$ . Therefore,  $FBXD$  is cyclic.

It follows that  $\angle XBC = \angle XDF \equiv \angle XDA$ . By symmetry, this means that  $\angle XAB = \angle XCD$  as well so  $X$  is the point described in the problem and  $(\angle BXA, \angle CXD)$  are supplementary as required.

First note that the point \(X\) is unique: if \(P=\overline{AB}\cap\overline{CD}\) and \(Q=\overline{AD}\cap\overline{BC}\), we have \(\measuredangle XBQ=\measuredangle XBC=\measuredangle XDA=\measuredangle XDQ\), so \(X\) lies on \((BDQ)\), and similarly \(X\) lies on \((ACP)\). Since \(X\) lies in the interior of \(ABCD\), this uniquely determines \(X\).

Now we describe \(X\). Let \(R=\overline{AC}\cap\overline{BD}\). Since \(AB/AD=CB/CD\), by Steiner we may find a common isogonal foot \(K\) of \(R\) in \(\triangle ABD\) and \(\triangle CBD\), i.e.\ so that \(\measuredangle CAB=\measuredangle DAK\) and \(\measuredangle BCA=\measuredangle KCD\).

Let the circumcircles of \(\triangle AKB\) and \(\triangle CKD\) intersect at \(X'\). We have \begin{align*}     \measuredangle BX'D=\measuredangle BX'K+\measuredangle KX'D&=\measuredangle BAK+\measuredangle KCD     &=\measuredangle CAD+\measuredangle BCA=\measuredangle CQA, \end{align*} so \(X'\in(BDQ)\). Analogously \(X'\in(ACP)\), so \(X=X'\).
