We claim that answer is  $f \equiv 1$ , which is easily checked. Consider taking  $x = \frac{1}{f(y)}$  to fnd,
\begin{align*}
f(1) &= f(y) \cdot f\left(\frac{1}{f(y)} \right)^2
\end{align*}
Plugging in  $y = 1$  this means,
\begin{align*}
f\left( \frac{1}{f(1)} \right) = 1
\end{align*}
However we may also note if we take  $y = \frac{1}{f(1)}$ ,
\begin{align*}
f(x^2) = f(x)^2
\end{align*}
From here take this identity and plug it back into the original equation to find,
\begin{align*}
f(xf(y))^2 &= f(x)^2f(y) \iff \frac{f(xf(y))^2}{f(x)^2} = f(y)
\end{align*}
Then  $f(x)$  is always the square of a rational number. However then as,
\begin{align*}
f(xf(y)) = f(x)\sqrt{f(y)}
\end{align*}
we require  $f(y)$  to always be the fourth power of a rational number. However then this implies,
\begin{align*}
\frac{f(xf(y))}{f(x)} = \sqrt{f(y)}
\end{align*}
which in turn gives  $\sqrt{f(y)}$  is the perfect fourth power of a rational, which forces  $f(y)$  to be a perfect eighth power of a rational. Continuing this infinite descent  $f(y)$  is a perfect power of a rational for each of the powers of  $2$ . This forces  $f(y) = 1$  as desired.

