If  $n=3$  then we can use the set  $\{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7\}$ . Now assume  $n\ge 4$ . Let  $\text{Sum}(R)$  for some set  $R$  denote the sum of the elements in  $R$ . Now assume that we want to prove that we can satisfy find some set for some given  $n$ . Let  $k=\frac{n(n+1)}{2}$ . Then let
\begin{align*}
    &A = \{\text{Sum}(B), k\} 
    &B = \left\{k - \frac{1(2)}{2}, k-\frac{2(3)}{2}, \dots, k-\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}{2}\right\} 
    &C = \{n, n-1, n-2, \dots, 1\}.
\end{align*}
We will show that \[ S=A\cup B\cup C \] satisfies the given condition. It is easy to see that  $\text{Sum}(A)=\text{Sum}(B)+\text{Sum}(C)$ . Now we use an induction-like approach to show that we can satisfy the condition. For the  $m=2$  case, we can just have the subset with  $2$  elements be the set  $A$ . Then if we wanted to go to  $m=3$ , we can replace  $k$  with the first element of  $B$  combined with the last element of  $C$ . So we replace  $k$  with  $k-\frac{1(2)}{2}$  and  $1$ . Then if we want to go from  $m=3$  to  $m=4$  we replace  $k-\frac{1(2)}{2}$  with the second element from  $B$  and the second to last element from  $C$ . So we replace  $k-\frac{1(2)}{2}$  with  $k-\frac{2(3)}{2}$  and  $2$ . Then so on, replacing the next number with the third element form  $B$  and the third to last element from  $C$ . The first steps of the process would look something like this:
\begin{align*}
    &\{ \text{Sum}(B), k \} 
    &\{ \text{Sum}(B), k-\frac{1(2)}{2}, 1 \} 
    &\{ \text{Sum}(B), k - \frac{2(3)}{2}, 2, 1 \} 
    &\{ \text{Sum}(B), k - \frac{3(4)}{2}, 3, 2, 1 \} 
    &\{ \text{Sum}(B), k - \frac{(4)(5)}{2}, 4, 3, 2, 1 \}
\end{align*}
If we continue this process we will eventually get to the set \[ \left\{\text{Sum}(B), k - \frac{(n-2)(n-1)}{2}, n-2, n-3, \dots, 1\right\} \] which has exactly  $n$  elements, concluding our proof.

Simply consider the set  $S={f_1,f_2,\dots,f_{2n}}$  where  $f_i$  is the  $i^{th}$  number in the recursion;  $$ f_1 =1 \ \ \ f_2 = 2\ \ \ f_{n+2}=f_{n+1}+f_n  $$  This is just the Fibonacci Sequence starting from 1 and 2. Now, notice that we can have
\begin{align*}
    \{f_1,f_2,\dots,f_{2n-2}\} &\text{ and } \{f_{2n-1},f_{2n}\}
    \{f_1,f_2,\dots,f_{2n-4},f_{2n-1}\} &\text{ and } \{f_{2n-3},f_{2n-2},f_{2n}\}
    &\vdots
    \{f_1,f_2,f_5\dots,f_{2n-1}\} &\text{ and } \{f_3,f_4,\dots,f_{2n}\}
\end{align*}
Thus, for all  $n\geq 3$ , there exists a set  $S$  of  $2n$  distinct positive integers such that for any  $m \in \{2,\dots,n\}$  the set  $S$  can be partitioned into two sets with cardinalities  $m$  and  $2n-m$  with equal sums.

If  $n = 3$ , then we can let  $S = \{1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 7\}$  whereupon we can choose the subsets \[\{5, 7\}, \{1, 2, 3, 6\}\] and \[\{1, 5, 6\}, \{2, 3, 7\}\] for  $m = 2$  and  $3$  respectively.

Else let  $S$  be \[\left\{3^{n - 1}, \frac{3^{n - 1} - 3}{2}\right\} \cup \bigcup_{0 \leq i \leq n - 2} \{3^i, 2 \cdot 3^i\}\text{.}\] This is a well-defined set since
\begin{align*}
    \frac{3^{n - 1} - 3}{2} &= \frac{3(3^{n - 2} - 1)}{2} 
    &= 3(1 + 3 + \dots + 3^{n - 3}) 
    &= 3 + 3^2 + \dots + 3^{n - 2}
\end{align*}
and since  $n - 2 \geq 2$ , this sum has at least two powers of  $3$  and is thus not equal to any other element in  $S$  by comparing their base- $3$  representations.

Now we show that this choice of  $S$  actually works. This argument is clearer if we let  $n$  be, say,  $5$ :

We start with the two subsets \[\left\{3^4, \frac{3^4 - 3}{2}\right\}, \{1, 2, 3, 2 \cdot 3, 3^2, 2 \cdot 3^2, 3^3, 2 \cdot 3^3\}\] which evidently work. Now we swap the  $3^4$  on the left-hand side with the  $3^3$  and  $2 \cdot 3^3$  on the right-hand side:
\[\left\{3^3, 2 \cdot 3^3, \frac{3^4 - 3}{2}\right\}, \{1, 2, 3, 2 \cdot 3, 3^2, 2 \cdot 3^2, 3^4\}\text{.}\] This still works since  $3^4 = 3^3 + 2 \cdot 3^3$ . Now we swap the  $3^3$  on the left-hand side with the  $3^2$  and  $2 \cdot 3^2$  on the right-hand side again. This argument can continue until we reach the sets
\[\left\{3, 2 \cdot 3, 2 \cdot 3^2, 2 \cdot 3^3, \frac{3^4 - 3}{2}\right\}, \{1, 2, 3^2, 3^3, 3^4\}\] at which point we're done. (We can actually apply the argument again, but we don't need to.)

Consider the set  $\{F_1, F_2, F_3, \dots, F_{2n-1}, \sigma - F_{2n-1}\}$  where we define  $\sigma = F_1 + F_2 + \dots + F_{2n-2}$ . Then consider,
\begin{align*}
S_2 &= \{\sigma - F_{2n - 1}, F_{2n - 1}\}
S_3 &= \{\sigma - F_{2n - 1}, F_{2n - 2}, F_{2n - 3}\}
S_4 &= \{\sigma - F_{2n - 1}, F_{2n - 2}, F_{2n - 4}, F_{2n - 5}\}
&\vdots
S_n &= \{\sigma - F_{2n - 1}, F_{2n - 2}, F_{2n - 4}, F_{2n - 6}, \dots, F_{4}, F_3 \}
\end{align*}
Clearly each of these partitions work so we're done.  $\square$

