Note that \begin{align*}\angle O_BOO_C&=\angle O_BOP+\angle POO_C=\frac12 (\angle BOP+\angle POC)&=\frac12 \angle BOC=\angle BAC.\end{align*} But because  $O$  and  $P$  are symmetric about  $O_BO_C$ ,  $\angle O_BPO_C=\angle O_BOO_C=\angle BAC$ . Now the angle between  $\ell_B$  and  $\ell_C$  should be the same as the angle between  $AB$  and  $AC$ , so  $\angle O_BXO_C=\angle YXZ=\angle BAC=\angle O_BPO_C$ , implying  $O_BPXO_C$  is cyclic. Similarly,  $O_BPZO_A$  is cyclic.**Claim:**  $OP$  is tangent to the circumcircle of  $XPZ$  at  $P$ .**Proof**
We need to prove that  $\angle XPO=\angle XZP$ . Let  $H\in OP$  be the orthocenter of  $O_BOO_C$ . Then since  $\angle O_BHO_C=180^\circ-\angle O_BOO_C=180^\circ-\angle O_BPO_C$ ,  $PO_BHO_CX$  is cyclic, yielding  $\angle XPO=\angle XPH=\angle HO_BX$ . So it remains to show that   $$ \angle HO_BX=\angle XZP\iff O_BH \parallel PZ\iff OO_C\perp PZ. $$  Let  $\measuredangle (a,b)$  denoted the angle between the lines  $a,b$  measured in the clockwise direction. Then   $$ \measuredangle (OC,AC)=90^\circ-\angle B\implies \measuredangle(OC,XZ)=180^\circ-\angle B. $$  Also since  $\angle POO_C=\angle O_COC$ ,   $$ \measuredangle (OC,OO_C)=\frac12 \angle POC\implies \measuredangle (OO_C,XZ)=180^\circ-\angle B-\frac12\angle POC. $$  We also have
\begin{align*}
    \measuredangle (XZ,PZ)&=-\angle O_BZP=-\angle O_BO_AP
    &=-\angle OO_AO_B=\angle POO_A-90^\circ
    &=\frac12 \angle POA-90^\circ
    \implies \measuredangle (OO_C,PZ)&=180^\circ-\angle B-\frac12 \angle POC+\frac12\angle POA-90^\circ
    &= 90^\circ+\frac12(\angle POA-\angle POC-2\angle B)
    &= 90^\circ +\frac12(\angle AOC-2\angle B)=90^\circ
\end{align*}
because  $\angle AOC=2\angle B$ . Thus we obtain  $OO_C\perp PZ$ , proving the claim.  $\square$ In a similar manner we get  $OP$  is tangent to the circumcircle of  $XPY$  at  $P$ . This gives   $$ \angle XZP=\angle XPO=\angle XYP $$  implying  $XYZP$  are cyclic. Therefore  $OP$  is tangent to the circumcircle of  $XYZ$ , as desired.  $\blacksquare$</details>

In fact a shorter solution using angel chasing is possible.
<blockquote>Let  $O$  be the circumcentre, and  $\Omega$  be the circumcircle of an acute-angled triangle  $ABC$ . Let  $P$  be an arbitrary point on  $\Omega$ , distinct from  $A$ ,  $B$ ,  $C$ , and their antipodes in  $\Omega$ . Denote the circumcentres of the triangles  $AOP$ ,  $BOP$ , and  $COP$  by  $O_A$ ,  $O_B$ , and  $O_C$ , respectively. The lines  $\ell_A$ ,  $\ell_B$ ,  $\ell_C$  perpendicular to  $BC$ ,  $CA$ , and  $AB$  pass through  $O_A$ ,  $O_B$ , and  $O_C$ , respectively. Prove that the circumcircle of triangle formed by  $\ell_A$ ,  $\ell_B$ , and  $\ell_C$  is tangent to the line  $OP$ .</blockquote>
Let  $\ell_B \cap \ell_C=X.$  Define  $Y,Z$  similarly. Call the circle  $XYZ$  as  $\omega.$  Let  $S$  be the center of  $\omega.$  Also,  $K=\ell_A \cap BC,$  and  $L,M$  are similarly defined. We show that  $\omega$  is tangent to  $OP$  at  $P.$ **<span style="color:#00f">Claim:</span>**  The point  $P$  lies on  $\omega.$ *Proof:* Clearly, 
\begin{align*}
    \measuredangle O_CPO_A=\measuredangle O_CPO+\measuredangle OPO_A &=(\pi/2-\measuredangle OCP)+(\pi/2-\measuredangle PAO) 
    &=\measuredangle PAC+\measuredangle ACP 
    &=\measuredangle ABC =\measuredangle KYL
\end{align*}
Hence,  $PO_AYO_C$  is cyclic. Similarly,  $O_AO_BZP$  is cyclic. Thus, we are done by Miquel on  $XYO_AO_B.$   $\square$ [asy]
