<details><summary>solution</summary>Note that  $t_n=2t_{n-1}+t_{n-2}$  by induction: cover the first square with either green or red or cover the first two squares with blue. Also define  $t_0=1 $ .
Now note that  $t_{2n+1}=2t_{2n}+t_{2n-1}=t_1t_{2n}+t_0t_{2n-1}$  and suppose by induction that  $t_{2k+1}=t_at_{2k+1-a}+t_{a-1}t_{2k-a}$  for some  $k,a \in \mathbb{Z}^+$  holds. We induct on  $a$ .
Then \begin{align*}
    t_{2k+1}&=t_at_{2k+1-a}+t_{a-1}t_{2k-a} 
    &=t_a(t_1t_{2k-a}+t_{2k-1-a})+t_{a-1}t_{2k-a} 
    &=(t_1t_a+t_{a-1})t_{2k-a}+t_at_{2k-1-a} 
    &=t_{a+1}t_{2k-a}+t_at_{2k-1-a} \, ,
\end{align*}
This completes the induction. Hence we can write  $t_{2n+1}=t_{n+1}t_n+t_nt_{n-1}=t_n(t_{n+1}+t_{n-1})$ , which is clearly divisible by  $t_n$ .
This concludes the problem.  $\square$</details>

