The problem then gets transformed into the following: Given a triangle  $\triangle{A^*C^*D^*}$  and a point  $B$  in the interior of it such that its reflections over  $\overline{A^*C^*}$  and  $\overline{A^*D^*}$  lie on  $(A^*C^*D^*),$  prove that  $C^*D^*E^*F^*$  is cyclic, where   $$ E^*=C^*B \cap A^*D^*, F^*=D^*B \cap A^*C^*. $$   (This is due to the fact that when we invert around a vertex of a triangle, its circumcenter is mapped to the reflection of that vertex over the inverses of the other two vertices.)

By the converse of the Reflecting the Orthocenter lemma, it follows that  $B^*$  is the orthocenter of  $\triangle{A^*C^*D^*}$  in this new problem. This implies that  $E^*$  and  $F^*$  are the intersection points of  $\overline{BO_1^*}$  and  $\overline{BO_2^*}$  with the sides of the triangle. Therefore, in the original problem,  $E$  and  $F$  are the antipodes of  $B$  in the circles  $\Gamma_2$  and  $\Gamma_1,$  respectively. It results that
