Let  $H'$  be the reflection of  $H$  about line  $BC$ , since it's well-known that  $Pow_{FACD}(H)=AH\cdot HD=FH\cdot HC$ , we have  $KH\cdot HH'=\frac{AH}{2}\cdot 2HD=AH\cdot HD=FH\cdot HC$ , implying that quadrilateral  $FKCH'$  is cyclic. Moreover, since line  $PO$  is the perpendicular bisector of  $AH'$ ,  $\angle PH'A=\angle PAH'$ . We have 
\begin{align*}
\angle PH'C&=\angle PH'A+\angle AH'C  &=\angle PAH'+\angle ABC  &=\angle BAD+\angle ABD &=90^{\circ}  &=\angle PFC 
\end{align*}
so quadrilateral  $PFCH'$  is cyclic. Hence, points  $P,F,K,C$  and  $H'$  lie on one circle and so  $\angle PKC=\angle PFC=90^{\circ}. \quad \blacksquare$

 $\angle{AMB}=\angle{ADM}+\angle{BCM}$  implies that  $(AMD)$  and  $(BMC)$  are tangent at point  $B,$  and  $\angle{AMD} = \angle{ABM}+\angle{DM}$  implies that  $(AMB)$  and  $(DMC)$  are tangent at point  $B.$  Hence, inverting around  $B$  with radius  $1,$   $A^{*}B^{*}C^{*}D^{*}$  is a parallelogram with a point  $M$  in its interior. By the inversion distance formula, it suffices to prove that
\begin{align*}
    \frac{1}{A^{*}M \cdot C^{*}M} + \frac{1}{B^{*}M \cdot D^{*}M} \ge \sqrt{\frac{A^{*}B^{*} \cdot B^{*}C^{*} \cdot C^{*}D^{*} \cdot D^{*}A^{*}}{(MA^{*})^2(MB^{*})^2(MC^{*})^2(MD^{*})^2}}.
\end{align*}
Using the fact that  $A^{*}B^{*} = C^{*}D^{*}$  and  $B^{*}C^{*}=D^{*}A^{*},$  this can be manipulated into   $$ MA^{*} \cdot MC^{*} + MB^{*} \cdot MD^{*} \ge A^{*}B^{*} \cdot B^{*}C^{*}. $$   Translating  $\triangle{A^{*}MD^{*}}$  in the direction parallel to  $\overline{A^{*}B^{*}}$  and  $\overline{C^{*}D^{*}}$  such that  $A^{*}$  is mapped to  $B^{*}$  and  $D^{*}$  is mapped to  $C^{*},$  let  $M$  get mapped to  $M'.$  Then, the desired result is equivalent to Ptolemy's Inequality on quadrilateral  $MB^{*}M'C^{*},$  so we are done.  $\square$

We will provide a solution which does not use teh parity of  $n$ . Let the players be  $A,B$  where  $A$  starts. We claim that the game is a tie for  $n=2$  and otherwise  $B$  wins. Let the colours be  $X,O$ . When  $A$  plays on a number,  $B$  colours its match into the other colour. Pair  $(3i+1).$  column and  $(3i+2).,(3i+3).$  squares in each row. If  $n\equiv 2\pmod 3$ , then pair the last column, too.

\begin{tabular}{ c c c c c c c c} $1$  &  $2$  &  $2$  &  $4$  &  $5$  &  $5$  &  $7$  &  $\dots$  $1$  &  $3$  &  $3$  &  $4$  & $6$  &  $6$  &  $7$  &  $\dots$ \end{tabular}

We get  $n$  distinct pairs in each domino. Notice that if the dominoes between  $i.,(i+1).$  vertical dominoes are reflections, then they add  $2$  more distinct neighbours vertically and if they are the same, then they add  $2$  more different coloured neighbours one with  $i.$  and one with  $(i+1).$  domino. Hence we get at least  $5k-1,5k+1,5k+2$  different neighbours for  $3k,3k+1,3k+2$  which are at least  $\frac{3n-1}{2}$  hence  $B$  wins as desired. $\blacksquare$

