Let  $n$  be an integer such that, $qn\in\mathbb Z$ .Then,It is easy to show that  $z$  is aa root of  $z^{2n}-1=0$ .Hence , $z$  is a algebric integer.Similarly one can show  $z^{-1}$  is a algebric integer. So  $z+\frac{1}{z}=2\cos q\pi$  is  a algebric integer.
Since , $z+\frac{1}{z}=2\cos q\pi\in \mathbb Q$  so  $z$  is a rational integer.so it must be in  $\{0,\pm1,\pm2\}$ .  $\blacksquare$ **<span style="color:#f00">Lemma:</span>** Let  $n$  be an integer such that there exist a regular  $n$ -gon in the cartesian plane such that all of its vertices has integer lattices. Then  $n=4$ . 

